PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L05U6.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":5,"cefr":"B1a","unit":6,"theme":"Technology in Our Lives","essential_question":"How does technology help us, and when can it harm us?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["device","screen","app","online","password","digital","connect","download","privacy","robot","battery","update","safety","message"],"recycled_words":["phone","computer","game","family","rule","should","because","so","theme","character","persuade","sleep","safe"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS1","title":"Word Power: Technology in Our Lives","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about technology and 4 Tier 2 words (rely, access, balance, communicate)","recognize word families: a base word with prefixes and suffixes (connect → connection → connected → disconnect)","choose the correct word form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb) from its position in a sentence"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن التكنولوجيا و4 كلمات أكاديمية.","التعرّف على عائلات الكلمات: الكلمة الأصل مع السوابق واللواحق.","اختيار صيغة الكلمة الصحيحة (اسم، فعل، صفة، حال) من موقعها في الجملة."],"success_criteria":["I can use the technology words correctly in sentences.","I can build a word family from a base word and name the part of speech of each member.","I can choose the right form of a word by looking at the words around it."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات التكنولوجيا في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع بناء عائلة كلمات من كلمة أصل وتسمية نوع كل كلمة.","أستطيع اختيار الصيغة الصحيحة للكلمة بالنظر إلى الكلمات المحيطة بها."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"device","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈvaɪs/","definition":"a machine or tool made for a special job, such as a phone or a tablet","arabic":"جهاز","example":"Please turn off your device during the lesson.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"screen","pos":"noun","ipa":"/skrin/","definition":"the flat glass part of a phone, a TV or a computer where you see pictures and words","arabic":"شاشة","example":"Don't sit too close to the screen.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"app","pos":"noun","ipa":"/æp/","definition":"a program that you use on a phone or a tablet","arabic":"تطبيق","example":"My brother uses a reading app every night.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"online","pos":"adjective / adverb","ipa":"/ˌɑnˈlaɪn/","definition":"connected to the internet; on the internet","arabic":"متصل بالإنترنت / عبر الإنترنت","example":"We had an online lesson when it rained.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"password","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpæsˌwɝd/","definition":"a secret word or set of letters and numbers that lets you open an account","arabic":"كلمة المرور","example":"Never share your password with strangers.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"digital","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈdɪdʒɪtəl/","definition":"using computers and the internet","arabic":"رقمي","example":"Our school has a digital library.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"connect","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kəˈnɛkt/","definition":"to join two things together; to join the internet","arabic":"يوصل / يتصل","example":"Connect the tablet to the school Wi-Fi.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"download","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ˈdaʊnˌloʊd/","definition":"to copy a file or an app from the internet onto your device","arabic":"ينزّل (من الإنترنت)","example":"Ask a parent before you download a game.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"privacy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpraɪvəsi/","definition":"keeping your personal information away from other people","arabic":"الخصوصية","example":"Protect your privacy: don't post your address online.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"robot","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈroʊˌbɑt/","definition":"a machine that can move and do jobs by itself","arabic":"روبوت","example":"A robot cleans the floor in our house.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"battery","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbætəri/","definition":"the part of a device that stores power","arabic":"بطارية","example":"My phone battery is almost empty.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"update","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ˌʌpˈdeɪt/","definition":"to make a program newer and better; a newer version","arabic":"يحدّث / تحديث","example":"The tablet needs an update.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"safety","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈseɪfti/","definition":"being safe and protected from danger","arabic":"السلامة / الأمان","example":"Online safety is everyone's job.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"message","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɛsɪdʒ/","definition":"a short piece of writing or a voice note that you send to someone","arabic":"رسالة","example":"I sent my grandmother a voice message.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"rely","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈlaɪ/","definition":"to need or trust someone or something to help you","arabic":"يعتمد على","example":"Many people rely on maps on their phones.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"access","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈæksɛs/","definition":"the chance or the right to use or enter something; to get into","arabic":"وصول / يصل إلى","example":"Students have access to the computer lab.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"balance","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈbæləns/","definition":"a good, healthy mix of different things","arabic":"توازن / يوازن","example":"Keep a balance between screen time and outdoor play.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"communicate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kəˈmjunɪˌkeɪt/","definition":"to share ideas or information with someone","arabic":"يتواصل","example":"We communicate with our cousins by video call.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **word family** is a group of words that grow from the same **base word**. We add **suffixes** (endings) or **prefixes** (beginnings) to make new words with related meanings: **connect** (verb) → **connection** (noun) → **connected** (adjective) → **disconnect** (verb, opposite).\n\nSuffixes often tell you the **part of speech**: **-tion / -sion / -ment / -ity / -er / -or** → noun (*connection, protection, invention, inventor*); **-ed / -al / -ive / -able / -ful** → adjective (*connected, digital, creative, reliable, useful*); **-ly** → adverb (*safely, quickly*). Prefixes change the meaning: **dis-** = not / opposite (*disconnect*), **re-** = again (*reconnect, restart*), **un-** = not (*unsafe*).\n\nTo choose the right form, look at the **position**: after *a / the / my* → a **noun**; after *can / will / to* → a **verb**; after *is / feel / very* or before a noun → an **adjective**; describing **how** an action happens → an **adverb**.","examples":["**connect** (v) → **connection** (n) → **connected** (adj) → **disconnect** / **reconnect** (v)","**communicate** (v) → **communication** (n) → **communicator** (n, person)","**protect** (v) → **protection** (n) → **protective** (adj)","**safe** (adj) → **safety** (n) → **safely** (adv) → **unsafe** (adj)","**rely** (v) → **reliable** (adj) → **unreliable** (adj)","**invent** (v) → **invention** (n) → **inventor** (n, person) → **inventive** (adj)","Position: *The Wi-Fi **connection** is slow.* (after *the* → noun)","Position: *Please **connect** the printer.* (command → verb) · *Are you **connected**?* (after *are* → adjective)","Position: *Use your tablet **safely**.* (how? → adverb)"],"common_errors":["✗ The connect is slow today. → ✓ The connection is slow today. (After **the** → noun.)","✗ Please use the internet safe. → ✓ Please use the internet safely. (How? → adverb **-ly**.)","✗ My phone is not connection. → ✓ My phone is not connected. (After **is** → adjective.)","✗ Robots help in communicate. → ✓ Robots help in communication. (After a preposition → noun.)","✗ He is a good invention of apps. → ✓ He is a good inventor of apps. (A person → **-or**.)","✗ The app is unreliability. → ✓ The app is unreliable. (After **is** → adjective (**-able**).)","✗ I need to unconnect the charger. → ✓ I need to disconnect the charger. (The opposite of connect is **dis**connect.)"],"arabic":"عائلة الكلمات مجموعة كلمات تنشأ من كلمة أصل واحدة بإضافة لواحق أو سوابق، مثل: connect ← connection ← connected ← disconnect.\n\nاللواحق تدل غالبًا على نوع الكلمة: ‎-tion و‎-ment و‎-er/-or للأسماء، و‎-ed و‎-al و‎-ive و‎-able للصفات، و‎-ly للأحوال.\n\nالسوابق تغيّر المعنى: dis- للعكس، وre- للتكرار، وun- للنفي.\n\nاختر الصيغة من موقعها: بعد a/the اسم، وبعد can/to فعل، وبعد is أو قبل الاسم صفة، ولوصف كيفية الفعل حال."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Beep! Your device has a new message: welcome to Technology in Our Lives!","on_screen":"Technology in Our Lives 📱🤖","visual":"A phone screen lights up with a message icon; a small robot waves."},{"time":"00:25–01:20","narration":"Device, screen, app, online, password, digital, connect, download, privacy, robot, battery, update, safety, message!","on_screen":"device · screen · app · online · password · digital · connect · download · privacy · robot · battery · update · safety · message","visual":"Each word appears with an icon on a phone screen."},{"time":"01:20–01:55","narration":"We rely on technology to communicate, and we need access – but also balance!","on_screen":"rely · access · communicate · balance","visual":"A family on a video call; a scale with a tablet and a football."},{"time":"01:55–03:05","narration":"Meet the Connect family: connect, connection, connected, disconnect! Suffixes tell you the part of speech.","on_screen":"connect → connection → connected → disconnect","visual":"A family tree grows branches with word cards and colored suffix leaves."},{"time":"03:05–03:30","narration":"Look at the position, choose the right form, and your sentence clicks into place!","on_screen":"Position → right form ✔","visual":"A word card clicks into a sentence like a puzzle piece."}],"captions_ar":["تنبيه! وصلت رسالة جديدة إلى جهازك: مرحبًا بك في وحدة التكنولوجيا في حياتنا!","جهاز، شاشة، تطبيق، عبر الإنترنت، كلمة المرور، رقمي، يتصل، ينزّل، الخصوصية، روبوت، بطارية، تحديث، السلامة، رسالة!","نعتمد على التكنولوجيا للتواصل، ونحتاج إلى الوصول إليها، ولكن مع التوازن!","تعرّف على عائلة Connect: connect وconnection وconnected وdisconnect! اللواحق تخبرك بنوع الكلمة.","انظر إلى الموقع واختر الصيغة الصحيحة، فتكتمل جملتك!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, upbeat female voice, speed 0.95."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct technology word.","instructions_ar":"اختر كلمة التكنولوجيا الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"My tablet switched off because the ___ was empty.","answer":"battery","explanation":"Battery.","explanation_ar":"البطارية.","options":["password","privacy","message","battery"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"You need a secret ___ to open your school email account.","answer":"password","explanation":"Password.","explanation_ar":"كلمة المرور.","options":["screen","robot","update","password"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A ___ in the factory puts the caps on the water bottles all day.","answer":"robot","explanation":"Robot.","explanation_ar":"روبوت.","options":["robot","message","screen","password"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Before you ___ a new game, ask a parent for permission.","answer":"download","explanation":"Download.","explanation_ar":"تنزّل.","options":["rely","download","balance","charge"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"My phone shows a notice: \"A new ___ is ready. Install it to fix problems.\"","answer":"update","explanation":"Update.","explanation_ar":"تحديث.","options":["update","battery","privacy","device"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Posting your home address on a game chat is bad for your ___.","answer":"privacy","explanation":"Privacy.","explanation_ar":"الخصوصية.","options":["battery","screen","privacy","update"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Word families. Choose the correct form.","instructions_ar":"عائلات الكلمات: اختر الصيغة الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"The school Wi-Fi ___ is very slow this morning.","answer":"connection","explanation":"After *the* + noun before *is* → noun.","explanation_ar":"اسم.","options":["connect","connected","connection","connecting"],"dok":1,"skill":"word families","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Is your laptop ___ to the internet?","answer":"connected","explanation":"After *is* → adjective.","explanation_ar":"صفة.","options":["connection","connected","connect","connects"],"dok":2,"skill":"word families","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Always use online chat ___: never talk to strangers.","answer":"safely","explanation":"How? → adverb.","explanation_ar":"حال.","options":["safely","safe","safety","safer"],"dok":2,"skill":"word families","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word is the PERSON in the *invent* family of technology words?","answer":"inventor","explanation":"-or = person.","explanation_ar":"المخترع.","options":["invention","inventor","inventive","invented"],"dok":2,"skill":"word families","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word means *the opposite of connect* when you unplug a charger?","answer":"disconnect","explanation":"dis- = opposite.","explanation_ar":"disconnect.","options":["reconnect","unconnect","connected","disconnect"],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A good password gives your account ___ from hackers.","answer":"protection","explanation":"After *gives your account* → noun.","explanation_ar":"اسم.","options":["protect","protective","protection","protected"],"dok":3,"skill":"word families","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Many families ________ on video calls to see relatives who live far away.","answer":"rely","explanation":"Need/trust.","explanation_ar":"تعتمد.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Students have ________ to the digital library from home.","answer":"access","explanation":"Chance to use.","explanation_ar":"وصول.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Try to keep a healthy ________ between screen time and sports.","answer":"balance","explanation":"Good mix.","explanation_ar":"توازن.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Texting is one way to ________ with friends.","answer":"communicate","explanation":"Share ideas.","explanation_ar":"نتواصل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Good ________ between pilots and the airport keeps flights safe.","answer":"communication","explanation":"After good → noun.","explanation_ar":"التواصل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"This website is not ________; it often gives wrong facts.","answer":"reliable","explanation":"After is not → adjective.","explanation_ar":"موثوق.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات الجديدة.","items":[{"q":"Write three sentences with *connect*, *connection* and *connected*.","answer":"I connect my tablet to the TV. The connection is strong in the living room. Now the tablet is connected, and we can watch the lesson.","explanation":"Word family.","explanation_ar":"عائلة كلمات.","dok":2,"skill":"word families","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write two online-safety rules with *safely* and *privacy*.","answer":"Use chat apps safely and only with people you know. Protect your privacy by never sharing your address or school name.","explanation":"Rules.","explanation_ar":"قواعد.","dok":2,"skill":"apply","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which device do you rely on most? Why?","answer":"I rely on my tablet most because I use it for my reading app and to call my grandparents.","explanation":"Reflect.","explanation_ar":"تأمّل.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"},{"q":"Do you think robots will do most jobs in the future? Give one reason.","answer":"I think robots will do many dangerous jobs, such as cleaning tanks, but people will still be needed for jobs like teaching and caring for patients.","explanation":"Opinion.","explanation_ar":"رأي.","dok":3,"skill":"reasoning","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Family Reunion","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Family tree builder: the child drags word cards onto the right family tree and labels each as noun, verb, adjective or adverb; then fills sentence gaps with the correct form. Each correct tree lights up a 'Wi-Fi bar'.","Each student draws a word card.","Students walk around and find their family members.","Each family builds its tree on the board (verb, noun, adjective, adverb).","The teacher shows sentence-gap cards; families race to give the correct form.","Complete tree = 3 points; correct sentence = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *a program that you use on a phone or a tablet*?","answer":"app","explanation":"App.","explanation_ar":"تطبيق.","options":["battery","robot","screen","app"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *word family*?","answer":"a group of words that grow from the same base word","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمات.","options":["words that rhyme","words with opposite meanings","a group of words that grow from the same base word","words in alphabetical order"],"dok":1,"skill":"word families"},{"q":"*We **rely** on the GPS app to find new places.* *Rely on* means ___.","answer":"need and trust","explanation":"Rely.","explanation_ar":"نعتمد على.","options":["turn off","need and trust","forget about","laugh at"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which suffix often makes a NOUN, as in *connection* and *invention*?","answer":"-tion","explanation":"Suffix.","explanation_ar":"‎-tion.","options":["-ly","-tion","-ed","-ful"],"dok":1,"skill":"suffixes"},{"q":"Which sentence about the classroom tablet is correct?","answer":"The tablet is connected to the projector.","explanation":"After *is* → adjective.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The tablet is connected to the projector.","The tablet is connection to the projector.","The tablet is connect to the projector.","The tablet is connectly to the projector."],"dok":2,"skill":"word families"},{"q":"*Ride your bike ___ and wear a helmet.* Which form fits?","answer":"safely","explanation":"How? → adverb.","explanation_ar":"safely.","options":["safe","safety","safeness","safely"],"dok":2,"skill":"word families"},{"q":"What does the prefix *re-* mean in *reconnect* and *restart*?","answer":"again","explanation":"Prefix.","explanation_ar":"مرة أخرى.","options":["again","not","before","under"],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes"},{"q":"Which word is an ADJECTIVE in the *protect* family?","answer":"protective","explanation":"-ive = adjective.","explanation_ar":"صفة.","options":["protection","protect","protective","protector"],"dok":2,"skill":"word families"},{"q":"*My cousin spends ten hours a day on games and never plays outside.* What does he need?","answer":"a better balance","explanation":"Apply.","explanation_ar":"توازن أفضل.","options":["a better balance","a faster download","a new password","more battery"],"dok":3,"skill":"apply"},{"q":"Why is it useful to know word families when you read?","answer":"If you know one word, you can work out the meaning of its family members.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"تساعدك على فهم كلمات جديدة.","options":["It makes texts shorter.","If you know one word, you can work out the meaning of its family members.","It helps you skip new words.","Word families always rhyme."],"dok":3,"skill":"word strategies"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'device': a phone, a tablet and a smartwatch"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'screen': a child looking at a tablet screen"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'app': colorful app icons on a phone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'online': a student in an online class"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'password': a lock icon with stars ****"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'digital': an e-book on a tablet"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'connect': a cable joining a laptop and a screen"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'download': a download arrow on a screen"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'privacy': a closed curtain over a phone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'robot': a small robot vacuum"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'battery': a battery icon showing 10%"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'update': a circle arrow on a screen"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'safety': a shield on a laptop"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'message': a speech bubble on a phone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rely': a driver following a phone map"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'access': a key card opening a door"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'balance': a scale with a tablet and a football"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'communicate': two children waving on a video call"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-01","prompt":"A family using different kinds of technology at home."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for technology words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS1-03","prompt":"A word-family tree for connect."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V01","script":"device. Please turn off your device during the lesson."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V02","script":"screen. Don't sit too close to the screen."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V03","script":"app. My brother uses a reading app every night."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V04","script":"online. We had an online lesson when it rained."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V05","script":"password. Never share your password with strangers."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V06","script":"digital. Our school has a digital library."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V07","script":"connect. Connect the tablet to the school Wi-Fi."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V08","script":"download. Ask a parent before you download a game."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V09","script":"privacy. Protect your privacy: don't post your address online."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V10","script":"robot. A robot cleans the floor in our house."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V11","script":"battery. My phone battery is almost empty."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V12","script":"update. The tablet needs an update."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V13","script":"safety. Online safety is everyone's job."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V14","script":"message. I sent my grandmother a voice message."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V15","script":"rely. Many people rely on maps on their phones."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V16","script":"access. Students have access to the computer lab."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V17","script":"balance. Keep a balance between screen time and outdoor play."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-V18","script":"communicate. We communicate with our cousins by video call."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS1-VID","script":"Beep! Your device has a new message: welcome to Technology in Our Lives! Device, screen, app, online, password, digital, connect, download, privacy, robot, battery, update, safety, message! We rely on technology to communicate, and we need access – but also balance! Meet the Connect family: connect, connection, connected, disconnect! Suffixes tell you the part of speech. Look at the position, choose the right form, and your sentence clicks into place!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS2","title":"The Phone That Wouldn't Stop","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read a play of about 420 words about a boy and his new phone","use stage directions and dialogue to explain characters' feelings and actions","trace a problem, its consequences across the scenes and its solution, and explain how the main character changes"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة مسرحية من نحو 420 كلمة عن فتى وهاتفه الجديد.","استخدام الإرشادات المسرحية والحوار لشرح مشاعر الشخصيات وأفعالها.","تتبّع المشكلة ونتائجها عبر المشاهد وحلّها، وشرح كيف تتغير الشخصية الرئيسية."],"success_criteria":["I can explain what a stage direction shows about a character's feelings.","I can list the consequences of the problem in each scene with evidence.","I can explain the solution and how Yousef changes from the beginning to the end."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع شرح ما تُظهره الإرشادات المسرحية عن مشاعر الشخصية.","أستطيع ذكر نتائج المشكلة في كل مشهد بالدليل.","أستطيع شرح الحل وكيف تغيّر يوسف من البداية إلى النهاية."],"map_area":"Literary Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"notification","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌnoʊtəfəˈkeɪʃən/","definition":"a short alert on a screen that tells you about something new","arabic":"إشعار","example":"I turned off the game notifications.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"buzz","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/bʌz/","definition":"to make a low shaking sound, like a phone on silent","arabic":"يطنّ / يهتزّ","example":"The phone buzzed all night.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"ignore","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪɡˈnɔr/","definition":"to pay no attention to someone or something","arabic":"يتجاهل","example":"Don't ignore your sister when she talks to you.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"glow","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ɡloʊ/","definition":"a soft, steady light","arabic":"وهج / يتوهّج","example":"The glow of the screen lit up his face.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"yawn","pos":"verb","ipa":"/jɔn/","definition":"to open your mouth wide because you are tired","arabic":"يتثاءب","example":"Yousef yawned at the breakfast table.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"servant","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɝvənt/","definition":"a person or thing that works for and helps someone","arabic":"خادم","example":"A phone should be a servant, not a master.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"in charge","pos":"phrase","ipa":"/ɪn ˈtʃɑrdʒ/","definition":"having control of something and making the decisions","arabic":"المسؤول / المتحكّم","example":"You are in charge of your phone.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **play** tells a story through **dialogue** (what the characters say) and **stage directions** (the words in *italics* and brackets). Stage directions tell actors **what to do** and **how to feel**, so they are clues to the characters' feelings. *(without looking up)* shows that Yousef is not paying attention.\n\nMany plays show a **problem** and its **consequences** (results) growing from scene to scene. Track them: **Scene 1** – Yousef ignores his sister and his mother; **Scene 2** – he stays up after midnight; **Scene 3** – he is so tired that he pours juice into his cereal.\n\nThen find the **solution**: **who** helps, **what steps** solve the problem and **how the main character changes**. Ask: *How does Yousef act at the beginning? At the end? What made him change?* The change often shows the **theme**.","examples":["Stage direction: *(without looking up)* → Yousef is not listening to Sara.","Stage direction: *(rubbing his eyes)* → he is very tired.","Stage direction: *(quietly)* → he feels a little ashamed when he admits the phone is in charge.","Dialogue clue: *\"One minute, Mama!\"* + Narrator: *One minute became thirty.* → he is not keeping his promise.","Consequence in Scene 1: *Sara went to bed with her drawing still folded in her hand.* → his sister feels ignored.","Consequence in Scene 3: *He poured orange juice into his cereal bowl.* → he is too tired to think.","Solution steps: **turn off** game notifications → **use** bedtime mode → **charge** the phone in the kitchen.","Character change: **beginning** – the phone controls him; **end** – he is in charge and puts the phone in the basket first.","Theme: ***Technology should help us, not control us – people matter more than screens.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Reading the stage directions aloud as dialogue. → ✓ Stage directions are actions and feelings – act them, don't say them. (Italics = do, not say.)","✗ Yousef is a bad boy. → ✓ Yousef makes a mistake, but he learns and changes. (Characters can change.)","✗ Listing only one consequence. → ✓ Track a consequence in every scene. (Problem → consequences → solution.)","✗ The solution is that Grandpa takes the phone away. → ✓ Grandpa shows Yousef how to control the phone himself. (Check the evidence.)","✗ Yousef ignore his sister. → ✓ Yousef ignores his sister. (He/she → **-s**.)","✗ He was so tired that he pour juice. → ✓ He was so tired that he poured juice. (Past simple → **-ed**.)","✗ The theme is: phones. → ✓ The theme is: technology should help us, not control us. (A theme is a message, not a topic.)"],"arabic":"المسرحية تحكي القصة من خلال الحوار والإرشادات المسرحية (الكلمات المائلة بين قوسين)، وهذه الإرشادات تخبر الممثل بما يفعله وبما يشعر به، فهي أدلة على مشاعر الشخصيات.\n\nتتبّع المشكلة ونتائجها في كل مشهد: يتجاهل يوسف أخته وأمه، ثم يسهر بعد منتصف الليل، ثم يصب العصير على حبوب الإفطار من شدة التعب.\n\nابحث عن الحل: من ساعد؟ وما الخطوات؟ وكيف تغيّرت الشخصية؟ فالتغيّر يكشف غالبًا الفكرة العامة (الرسالة).\n\nانتبه: الموضوع كلمة (الهواتف)، أما الفكرة العامة فجملة كاملة (التكنولوجيا يجب أن تخدمنا لا أن تتحكم بنا)."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Buzz! Buzz! Ding! Who is in charge – you or your phone?","on_screen":"The Phone That Wouldn't Stop 📱","visual":"A phone buzzing across a sofa."},{"time":"00:25–01:25","narration":"Yousef, look at my drawing! … Nice, nice. Later, okay?","on_screen":"Scene 1","visual":"Animated living room: Yousef staring at his screen; Sara holding a drawing; Mama calling him to dinner."},{"time":"01:25–02:15","narration":"Just one more level… and one more message…","on_screen":"Scene 2 → Scene 3","visual":"A dark bedroom with a glowing screen; next morning, juice pours into a cereal bowl."},{"time":"02:15–03:00","narration":"A phone is a wonderful servant but a terrible master.","on_screen":"Notifications off · Bedtime mode · Charge in the kitchen","visual":"Grandpa shows three icons on the phone screen."},{"time":"03:00–03:30","narration":"Stage directions show feelings. Track the problem, its consequences and the solution!","on_screen":"Problem → Consequences → Solution","visual":"A basket labeled PHONES SLEEP HERE DURING DINNER."}],"captions_ar":["طنين! طنين! رنين! من المتحكّم: أنت أم هاتفك؟","يوسف، انظر إلى رسمتي! … جميل، جميل. لاحقًا، حسنًا؟","مستوى واحد آخر فقط… ورسالة واحدة أخرى…","الهاتف خادم رائع لكنه سيد سيئ.","الإرشادات المسرحية تُظهر المشاعر. تتبّع المشكلة ونتائجها والحل!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm male voice, speed 0.95. Separate voices for Yousef, Sara, Mama and Grandpa."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Phone That Wouldn't Stop","body":"**Characters:** Narrator, Yousef (11 years old), Sara (his sister, 7 years old), Mama, Grandpa Ibrahim\n\n**Scene 1:** The living room on a Thursday evening. Yousef's new phone lies on the sofa.\n\n**NARRATOR:** For his eleventh birthday, Yousef got his first phone. By Thursday, it had become his shadow.\n\n*(The phone buzzes. Buzz! Buzz! Yousef grabs it.)*\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(laughing at the screen)* Ha! Badr just sent the funniest video!\n\n**SARA:** *(holding up a paper)* Yousef, look at my drawing! I made it for you.\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(without looking up)* Nice, nice. Later, okay?\n\n*(Buzz! Ding! Buzz!)*\n\n**MAMA:** Yousef, dinner is ready. Please put the phone down.\n\n**YOUSEF:** One minute, Mama! I have to answer the group chat, or they'll think I'm ignoring them.\n\n**NARRATOR:** One minute became thirty. Sara went to bed with her drawing still folded in her hand.\n\n**Scene 2:** Yousef's bedroom, after midnight. The room is dark except for the glow of a screen.\n\n*(Buzz! The screen lights up Yousef's tired face.)*\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(yawning)* Just one more level… and one more message…\n\n**Scene 3:** The kitchen, the next morning.\n\n**NARRATOR:** The next morning, Yousef could hardly open his eyes. He poured orange juice into his cereal bowl.\n\n**GRANDPA:** *(raising an eyebrow)* Juice on cereal? That is a new recipe.\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(rubbing his eyes)* I'm so tired, Grandpa. My phone wouldn't stop. Every time I put it down, it buzzed again.\n\n**GRANDPA:** *(sitting down beside him)* A phone is a wonderful servant but a terrible master. Who is in charge – you or the phone?\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(quietly)* The phone, I think.\n\n**GRANDPA:** Then let's change that. May I show you something?\n\n*(Grandpa taps the screen.)*\n\n**GRANDPA:** First, turn off the notifications from games. Second, use bedtime mode, so the phone stays silent at night. Third, charge it in the kitchen, not next to your bed.\n\n**YOUSEF:** But what if my friends message me?\n\n**GRANDPA:** Real friends will still be there in the morning. *(smiling)* So will your family.\n\n*(Yousef looks at Sara, who is eating quietly.)*\n\n**YOUSEF:** Sara, can I see your drawing now?\n\n*(Sara unfolds the paper. It shows the family at dinner. Yousef's head is a phone.)*\n\n**SARA:** That's you.\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(laughing, then serious)* I don't want to be a phone with legs. Can you draw a new one this weekend? I'll sit with you the whole time.\n\n**SARA:** *(hugging him)* Deal!\n\n**NARRATOR:** That evening, a small basket appeared on the dinner table. On it, Yousef had written: PHONES SLEEP HERE DURING DINNER. His phone was the first one inside.","word_count":421,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U6-LS2-1","genre":"drama","role":"main","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[{"word":"notification","definition":"a short alert on a screen that tells you about something new","arabic":"إشعار"},{"word":"group chat","definition":"a conversation in an app with several people at the same time","arabic":"محادثة جماعية"},{"word":"bedtime mode","definition":"a phone setting that keeps it quiet and dark at night","arabic":"وضع النوم"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the play. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المسرحية واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Why did Yousef get his first phone?","answer":"It was a present for his eleventh birthday.","explanation":"Narrator, Scene 1.","explanation_ar":"هدية عيد ميلاده الحادي عشر.","options":["He won it in a competition.","Grandpa gave him his old phone.","It was a present for his eleventh birthday.","He bought it with his savings."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Sara want to show Yousef?","answer":"a drawing she made for him","explanation":"Scene 1.","explanation_ar":"رسمة رسمتها له.","options":["a drawing she made for him","a funny video","a new game","her homework"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did Yousef say he had to answer the group chat?","answer":"He thought his friends would think he was ignoring them.","explanation":"Scene 1.","explanation_ar":"خشي أن يظن أصدقاؤه أنه يتجاهلهم.","options":["His teacher asked a question.","Mama sent him a message.","He wanted to order dinner.","He thought his friends would think he was ignoring them."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where does Scene 2 take place?","answer":"in Yousef's bedroom after midnight","explanation":"Scene heading.","explanation_ar":"في غرفة يوسف بعد منتصف الليل.","options":["in the kitchen in the morning","at school","in the living room at dinner","in Yousef's bedroom after midnight"],"dok":1,"skill":"setting","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where did Grandpa tell Yousef to charge his phone?","answer":"in the kitchen","explanation":"Scene 3.","explanation_ar":"في المطبخ.","options":["next to his bed","in the car","in the kitchen","at school"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Yousef write on the basket?","answer":"PHONES SLEEP HERE DURING DINNER","explanation":"Last line.","explanation_ar":"الهواتف تنام هنا أثناء العشاء.","options":["NO GAMES AFTER SCHOOL","PHONES SLEEP HERE DURING DINNER","SARA'S DRAWINGS","GRANDPA'S RULES"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Stage directions, consequences and change. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الإرشادات المسرحية والنتائج والتغيّر: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What does the stage direction *(without looking up)* show about Yousef?","answer":"He is not paying attention to Sara.","explanation":"Stage direction.","explanation_ar":"لا ينتبه إلى سارة.","options":["He is very happy with the drawing.","He is not paying attention to Sara.","He is looking for his shoes.","He is angry with Sara."],"dok":2,"skill":"stage directions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does the narrator mean by *One minute became thirty*?","answer":"Yousef stayed on his phone much longer than he promised.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"بقي على هاتفه أطول بكثير مما وعد.","options":["Yousef stayed on his phone much longer than he promised.","Dinner took thirty minutes to cook.","The clock was broken.","Yousef ran for thirty minutes."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which detail is a consequence of Yousef staying up late?","answer":"He poured orange juice into his cereal bowl.","explanation":"Cause → effect.","explanation_ar":"صب العصير على الحبوب.","options":["Sara made a drawing.","Badr sent a video.","Mama made dinner.","He poured orange juice into his cereal bowl."],"dok":2,"skill":"consequences","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How does Sara probably feel when she goes to bed with her drawing still folded?","answer":"ignored and sad","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"متجاهَلة وحزينة.","options":["proud and excited","angry with Mama","ignored and sad","sleepy but happy"],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does Grandpa mean by *a wonderful servant but a terrible master*?","answer":"A phone is great when it helps us, but bad when it controls us.","explanation":"Figurative language.","explanation_ar":"يفيد حين يخدمنا ويضر حين يتحكم بنا.","options":["A phone is great when it helps us, but bad when it controls us.","Phones should do our housework.","Servants should not use phones.","Old phones are better than new ones."],"dok":3,"skill":"figurative language","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does the stage direction *(laughing, then serious)* show about Yousef when he sees Sara's drawing?","answer":"He finds it funny at first, then he understands the message.","explanation":"Stage direction.","explanation_ar":"يضحك ثم يدرك الرسالة.","options":["He thinks the drawing is badly drawn.","He is pretending to be sad.","He is laughing at Grandpa.","He finds it funny at first, then he understands the message."],"dok":3,"skill":"stage directions","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the line that shows Yousef admits the phone is in charge.","answer":"The phone, I think.","explanation":"Admits.","explanation_ar":"يعترف.","evidence":"The phone, I think.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L05-U6-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["**Characters:** Narrator, Yousef (11 years old), Sara (his sister, 7 years old), Mama, Grandpa Ibrahim","**Scene 1:** The living room on a Thursday evening.","Yousef's new phone lies on the sofa.","**NARRATOR:** For his eleventh birthday, Yousef got his first phone.","By Thursday, it had become his shadow.","*(The phone buzzes.","Buzz!","Buzz!","Yousef grabs it.)*","**YOUSEF:** *(laughing at the screen)* Ha!","Badr just sent the funniest video!","**SARA:** *(holding up a paper)* Yousef, look at my drawing!","I made it for you.","**YOUSEF:** *(without looking up)* Nice, nice.","Later, okay?","*(Buzz!","Ding!","Buzz!)*","**MAMA:** Yousef, dinner is ready.","Please put the phone down.","**YOUSEF:** One minute, Mama!","I have to answer the group chat, or they'll think I'm ignoring them.","**NARRATOR:** One minute became thirty.","Sara went to bed with her drawing still folded in her hand.","**Scene 2:** Yousef's bedroom, after midnight.","The room is dark except for the glow of a screen.","*(Buzz!","The screen lights up Yousef's tired face.)*","**YOUSEF:** *(yawning)* Just one more level… and one more message…","**Scene 3:** The kitchen, the next morning.","**NARRATOR:** The next morning, Yousef could hardly open his eyes.","He poured orange juice into his cereal bowl.","**GRANDPA:** *(raising an eyebrow)* Juice on cereal?","That is a new recipe.","**YOUSEF:** *(rubbing his eyes)* I'm so tired, Grandpa.","My phone wouldn't stop.","Every time I put it down, it buzzed again.","**GRANDPA:** *(sitting down beside him)* A phone is a wonderful servant but a terrible master.","Who is in charge – you or the phone?","**YOUSEF:** *(quietly)* The phone, I think.","**GRANDPA:** Then let's change that.","May I show you something?","*(Grandpa taps the screen.)*","**GRANDPA:** First, turn off the notifications from games.","Second, use bedtime mode, so the phone stays silent at night.","Third, charge it in the kitchen, not next to your bed.","**YOUSEF:** But what if my friends message me?","**GRANDPA:** Real friends will still be there in the morning. *(smiling)* So will your family.","*(Yousef looks at Sara, who is eating quietly.)*","**YOUSEF:** Sara, can I see your drawing now?","*(Sara unfolds the paper.","It shows the family at dinner.","Yousef's head is a phone.)*","**SARA:** That's you.","**YOUSEF:** *(laughing, then serious)* I don't want to be a phone with legs.","Can you draw a new one this weekend?","I'll sit with you the whole time.","**SARA:** *(hugging him)* Deal!","**NARRATOR:** That evening, a small basket appeared on the dinner table.","On it, Yousef had written: PHONES SLEEP HERE DURING DINNER.","His phone was the first one inside."]},{"q":"Underline the narrator's line that proves Yousef has changed at the end.","answer":"His phone was the first one inside.","explanation":"Change.","explanation_ar":"التغيّر.","evidence":"His phone was the first one inside.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L05-U6-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"freer","sentences":["**Characters:** Narrator, Yousef (11 years old), Sara (his sister, 7 years old), Mama, Grandpa Ibrahim","**Scene 1:** The living room on a Thursday evening.","Yousef's new phone lies on the sofa.","**NARRATOR:** For his eleventh birthday, Yousef got his first phone.","By Thursday, it had become his shadow.","*(The phone buzzes.","Buzz!","Buzz!","Yousef grabs it.)*","**YOUSEF:** *(laughing at the screen)* Ha!","Badr just sent the funniest video!","**SARA:** *(holding up a paper)* Yousef, look at my drawing!","I made it for you.","**YOUSEF:** *(without looking up)* Nice, nice.","Later, okay?","*(Buzz!","Ding!","Buzz!)*","**MAMA:** Yousef, dinner is ready.","Please put the phone down.","**YOUSEF:** One minute, Mama!","I have to answer the group chat, or they'll think I'm ignoring them.","**NARRATOR:** One minute became thirty.","Sara went to bed with her drawing still folded in her hand.","**Scene 2:** Yousef's bedroom, after midnight.","The room is dark except for the glow of a screen.","*(Buzz!","The screen lights up Yousef's tired face.)*","**YOUSEF:** *(yawning)* Just one more level… and one more message…","**Scene 3:** The kitchen, the next morning.","**NARRATOR:** The next morning, Yousef could hardly open his eyes.","He poured orange juice into his cereal bowl.","**GRANDPA:** *(raising an eyebrow)* Juice on cereal?","That is a new recipe.","**YOUSEF:** *(rubbing his eyes)* I'm so tired, Grandpa.","My phone wouldn't stop.","Every time I put it down, it buzzed again.","**GRANDPA:** *(sitting down beside him)* A phone is a wonderful servant but a terrible master.","Who is in charge – you or the phone?","**YOUSEF:** *(quietly)* The phone, I think.","**GRANDPA:** Then let's change that.","May I show you something?","*(Grandpa taps the screen.)*","**GRANDPA:** First, turn off the notifications from games.","Second, use bedtime mode, so the phone stays silent at night.","Third, charge it in the kitchen, not next to your bed.","**YOUSEF:** But what if my friends message me?","**GRANDPA:** Real friends will still be there in the morning. *(smiling)* So will your family.","*(Yousef looks at Sara, who is eating quietly.)*","**YOUSEF:** Sara, can I see your drawing now?","*(Sara unfolds the paper.","It shows the family at dinner.","Yousef's head is a phone.)*","**SARA:** That's you.","**YOUSEF:** *(laughing, then serious)* I don't want to be a phone with legs.","Can you draw a new one this weekend?","I'll sit with you the whole time.","**SARA:** *(hugging him)* Deal!","**NARRATOR:** That evening, a small basket appeared on the dinner table.","On it, Yousef had written: PHONES SLEEP HERE DURING DINNER.","His phone was the first one inside."]},{"q":"Why does Sara draw Yousef with a phone for a head?","answer":"to show that he is always looking at his phone and not at his family","explanation":"Symbol.","explanation_ar":"لتُظهر أنه دائمًا على هاتفه.","options":["because she cannot draw faces","to show that he is always looking at his phone and not at his family","because Yousef asked her to","to sell the drawing"],"dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Which is the BEST statement of the theme of *The Phone That Wouldn't Stop*?","answer":"Technology should help us, not control us, and people matter more than screens.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"الفكرة العامة.","options":["Phones are expensive.","Grandparents know how to cook.","Technology should help us, not control us, and people matter more than screens.","Group chats are always bad."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain consequences, solutions and change.","instructions_ar":"اشرح النتائج والحلول والتغيّر.","items":[{"q":"List one consequence of Yousef's phone problem in each scene.","answer":"Scene 1: he ignores Sara and is late for dinner. Scene 2: he stays awake after midnight. Scene 3: he is so tired that he pours juice into his cereal.","explanation":"Consequences.","explanation_ar":"النتائج.","dok":2,"skill":"consequences","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain Grandpa's three steps in your own words.","answer":"Yousef should stop game alerts, make the phone silent at night and leave it to charge in the kitchen so it is not near his bed.","explanation":"Solution.","explanation_ar":"الحل.","dok":2,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"production"},{"q":"How does Yousef change from the beginning to the end? Give evidence from both parts.","answer":"At the beginning, the phone controls him: he says 'Later, okay?' to Sara without looking up. At the end, he is in charge: he promises to sit with Sara and puts his phone in the basket first.","explanation":"Change.","explanation_ar":"التغيّر.","dok":3,"skill":"character change","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which of Grandpa's steps would help YOU or your family most? Why?","answer":"Charging phones in the kitchen would help my family, because we would sleep better and talk more in the evening.","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"ربط.","dok":3,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"The Phone That Wouldn't Stop\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “The Phone That Wouldn't Stop”.","answer":"421 words","title":"The Phone That Wouldn't Stop","body":"**Characters:** Narrator, Yousef (11 years old), Sara (his sister, 7 years old), Mama, Grandpa Ibrahim\n\n**Scene 1:** The living room on a Thursday evening. Yousef's new phone lies on the sofa.\n\n**NARRATOR:** For his eleventh birthday, Yousef got his first phone. By Thursday, it had become his shadow.\n\n*(The phone buzzes. Buzz! Buzz! Yousef grabs it.)*\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(laughing at the screen)* Ha! Badr just sent the funniest video!\n\n**SARA:** *(holding up a paper)* Yousef, look at my drawing! I made it for you.\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(without looking up)* Nice, nice. Later, okay?\n\n*(Buzz! Ding! Buzz!)*\n\n**MAMA:** Yousef, dinner is ready. Please put the phone down.\n\n**YOUSEF:** One minute, Mama! I have to answer the group chat, or they'll think I'm ignoring them.\n\n**NARRATOR:** One minute became thirty. Sara went to bed with her drawing still folded in her hand.\n\n**Scene 2:** Yousef's bedroom, after midnight. The room is dark except for the glow of a screen.\n\n*(Buzz! The screen lights up Yousef's tired face.)*\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(yawning)* Just one more level… and one more message…\n\n**Scene 3:** The kitchen, the next morning.\n\n**NARRATOR:** The next morning, Yousef could hardly open his eyes. He poured orange juice into his cereal bowl.\n\n**GRANDPA:** *(raising an eyebrow)* Juice on cereal? That is a new recipe.\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(rubbing his eyes)* I'm so tired, Grandpa. My phone wouldn't stop. Every time I put it down, it buzzed again.\n\n**GRANDPA:** *(sitting down beside him)* A phone is a wonderful servant but a terrible master. Who is in charge – you or the phone?\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(quietly)* The phone, I think.\n\n**GRANDPA:** Then let's change that. May I show you something?\n\n*(Grandpa taps the screen.)*\n\n**GRANDPA:** First, turn off the notifications from games. Second, use bedtime mode, so the phone stays silent at night. Third, charge it in the kitchen, not next to your bed.\n\n**YOUSEF:** But what if my friends message me?\n\n**GRANDPA:** Real friends will still be there in the morning. *(smiling)* So will your family.\n\n*(Yousef looks at Sara, who is eating quietly.)*\n\n**YOUSEF:** Sara, can I see your drawing now?\n\n*(Sara unfolds the paper. It shows the family at dinner. Yousef's head is a phone.)*\n\n**SARA:** That's you.\n\n**YOUSEF:** *(laughing, then serious)* I don't want to be a phone with legs. Can you draw a new one this weekend? I'll sit with you the whole time.\n\n**SARA:** *(hugging him)* Deal!\n\n**NARRATOR:** That evening, a small basket appeared on the dinner table. On it, Yousef had written: PHONES SLEEP HERE DURING DINNER. His phone was the first one inside.","words":421,"limit":267}],"time_limit_sec":267},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Problem–Consequence–Solution Chart","instructions":"Organizer: Write the problem, one consequence from each scene, the solution and how Yousef changes. Add evidence.","items":[{"q":"Problem","answer":"Yousef's new phone controls him; it never stops buzzing."},{"q":"Consequence – Scene 1","answer":"He ignores Sara's drawing and is late for dinner."},{"q":"Consequence – Scene 2","answer":"He stays up after midnight playing and messaging."},{"q":"Consequence – Scene 3","answer":"He is so tired that he pours juice into his cereal."},{"q":"Solution (three steps)","answer":"turn off game notifications; use bedtime mode; charge the phone in the kitchen"},{"q":"How Yousef changes","answer":"from controlled by the phone → in charge; he gives time to Sara and puts his phone in the basket first"}]}],"game":{"name":"Stage Direction Charades","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Act and guess: an animated character performs a stage direction; the child chooses the feeling it shows and the line it matches. Correct answers fill a phone battery from 0% to 100%.","A player draws a stage-direction card and acts it without words.","The team guesses the stage direction within 30 seconds.","Then the team says what feeling it shows and which character does it.","Bonus: say the line that goes with it.","Correct guess = 2 points; correct feeling = 1 point; correct line = 1 bonus point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What are *stage directions* in a play?","answer":"words in italics that tell actors what to do and how to feel","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الإرشادات المسرحية.","options":["the names of the actors","the title of the play","words in italics that tell actors what to do and how to feel","the lines the narrator says"],"dok":1,"skill":"drama terms"},{"q":"What is a *notification*?","answer":"a short alert on a screen that tells you about something new","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"إشعار.","options":["a short alert on a screen that tells you about something new","a kind of battery","a phone case","a password"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Don't **ignore** your sister when she talks to you.* *Ignore* means ___.","answer":"pay no attention to","explanation":"Ignore.","explanation_ar":"يتجاهل.","options":["pay no attention to","listen carefully to","draw a picture for","call on the phone"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"How many scenes does *The Phone That Wouldn't Stop* have?","answer":"three","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"ثلاثة.","options":["two","four","one","three"],"dok":1,"skill":"structure"},{"q":"Which sentence about Yousef and Sara is correct?","answer":"Yousef ignores his sister in Scene 1.","explanation":"He → -s.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Yousef ignore his sister in Scene 1.","Yousef ignoring his sister in Scene 1.","Yousef ignores his sister in Scene 1.","Yousef are ignoring his sister in Scene 1."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What does *(yawning)* in Scene 2 tell the actor playing Yousef?","answer":"to show that Yousef is very sleepy","explanation":"Stage direction.","explanation_ar":"أن يُظهر النعاس.","options":["to sing a song","to show that Yousef is very sleepy","to shout at Sara","to leave the stage"],"dok":2,"skill":"stage directions"},{"q":"What is the MAIN problem in the play about Yousef's phone?","answer":"His new phone controls his time and attention.","explanation":"Problem.","explanation_ar":"الهاتف يتحكم بوقته وانتباهه.","options":["His phone is broken.","He has no friends.","Sara takes his phone.","His new phone controls his time and attention."],"dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution"},{"q":"Which detail shows the solution is working at the end of the play?","answer":"Yousef puts his phone in the dinner basket first.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"يضع هاتفه في السلة أولًا.","options":["Badr sends another video.","Yousef puts his phone in the dinner basket first.","Sara folds her drawing.","Grandpa raises an eyebrow."],"dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution"},{"q":"Why does Grandpa say *Real friends will still be there in the morning*?","answer":"to show Yousef he doesn't need to answer every message at night","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"ليطمئنه أنه لا يحتاج إلى الرد ليلًا.","options":["to tell Yousef his friends are moving away","to show Yousef he doesn't need to answer every message at night","because he wants to meet Badr","because the internet stops at night"],"dok":3,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"How does Yousef change during the play?","answer":"He goes from being controlled by his phone to being in charge of it and caring for his family.","explanation":"Character change.","explanation_ar":"يصبح هو المتحكّم.","options":["He goes from loving his sister to ignoring her.","He decides to sell his phone.","He stops talking to Grandpa.","He goes from being controlled by his phone to being in charge of it and caring for his family."],"dok":3,"skill":"character change"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'notification': a phone screen full of red alert bubbles"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'buzz': a phone shaking on a table with sound lines"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ignore': a boy looking at a phone while a girl waves"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'glow': a face lit by a phone in a dark room"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'yawn': a boy yawning"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'servant': a robot carrying a tray"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'in charge': a boy holding a phone with a confident smile"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-01","prompt":"Yousef ignores his sister's drawing while looking at his phone."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-02","prompt":"Tired Yousef pours juice into his cereal."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS2-03","prompt":"A basket for phones on the dinner table."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V01","script":"notification. I turned off the game notifications."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V02","script":"buzz. The phone buzzed all night."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V03","script":"ignore. Don't ignore your sister when she talks to you."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V04","script":"glow. The glow of the screen lit up his face."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V05","script":"yawn. Yousef yawned at the breakfast table."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V06","script":"servant. A phone should be a servant, not a master."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-V07","script":"in charge. You are in charge of your phone."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-T1","script":"**Characters:** Narrator, Yousef (11 years old), Sara (his sister, 7 years old), Mama, Grandpa Ibrahim **Scene 1:** The living room on a Thursday evening. Yousef's new phone lies on the sofa. **NARRATOR:** For his eleventh birthday, Yousef got his first phone. By Thursday, it had become his shadow. *(The phone buzzes. Buzz! Buzz! Yousef grabs it.)* **YOUSEF:** *(laughing at the screen)* Ha! Badr just sent the funniest video! **SARA:** *(holding up a paper)* Yousef, look at my drawing! I made it for you. **YOUSEF:** *(without looking up)* Nice, nice. Later, okay? *(Buzz! Ding! Buzz!)* **MAMA:** Yousef, dinner is ready. Please put the phone down. **YOUSEF:** One minute, Mama! I have to answer the group chat, or they'll think I'm ignoring them. **NARRATOR:** One minute became thirty. Sara went to bed with her drawing still folded in her hand. **Scene 2:** Yousef's bedroom, after midnight. The room is dark except for the glow of a screen. *(Buzz! The screen lights up Yousef's tired face.)* **YOUSEF:** *(yawning)* Just one more level… and one more message… **Scene 3:** The kitchen, the next morning. **NARRATOR:** The next morning, Yousef could hardly open his eyes. He poured orange juice into his cereal bowl. **GRANDPA:** *(raising an eyebrow)* Juice on cereal? That is a new recipe. **YOUSEF:** *(rubbing his eyes)* I'm so tired, Grandpa. My phone wouldn't stop. Every time I put it down, it buzzed again. **GRANDPA:** *(sitting down beside him)* A phone is a wonderful servant but a terrible master. Who is in charge – you or the phone? **YOUSEF:** *(quietly)* The phone, I think. **GRANDPA:** Then let's change that. May I show you something? *(Grandpa taps the screen.)* **GRANDPA:** First, turn off the notifications from games. Second, use bedtime mode, so the phone stays silent at night. Third, charge it in the kitchen, not next to your bed. **YOUSEF:** But what if my friends message me? **GRANDPA:** Real friends will still be there in the morning. *(smiling)* So will your family. *(Yousef looks at Sara, who is eating quietly.)* **YOUSEF:** Sara, can I see your drawing now? *(Sara unfolds the paper. It shows the family at dinner. Yousef's head is a phone.)* **SARA:** That's you. **YOUSEF:** *(laughing, then serious)* I don't want to be a phone with legs. Can you draw a new one this weekend? I'll sit with you the whole time. **SARA:** *(hugging him)* Deal! **NARRATOR:** That evening, a small basket appeared on the dinner table. On it, Yousef had written: PHONES SLEEP HERE DURING DINNER. His phone was the first one inside."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS2-VID","script":"Buzz! Buzz! Ding! Who is in charge – you or your phone? Yousef, look at my drawing! … Nice, nice. Later, okay? Just one more level… and one more message… A phone is a wonderful servant but a terrible master. Stage directions show feelings. Track the problem, its consequences and the solution!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS3","title":"Fact and opinion: \"Should Children Have Smartphones?\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["tell the difference between facts (can be checked) and opinions (beliefs or feelings)","find opinion signal words (I think, I believe, should, best, worst) and judge whether a fact supports an opinion","compare two writers' opinions and connect them to the play"],"objectives_ar":["التمييز بين الحقائق (يمكن التحقق منها) والآراء (معتقدات أو مشاعر).","إيجاد كلمات الرأي والحكم على ما إذا كانت الحقيقة تدعم الرأي.","مقارنة رأيي كاتبين وربطهما بالمسرحية."],"success_criteria":["I can sort sentences into facts and opinions and explain how I know.","I can find the facts each writer uses to support an opinion.","I can say which writer I agree with and support my choice with a fact."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تصنيف الجمل إلى حقائق وآراء وأشرح كيف عرفت.","أستطيع إيجاد الحقائق التي يستخدمها كل كاتب لدعم رأيه.","أستطيع أن أقول أيّ الكاتبين أوافق وأدعم اختياري بحقيقة."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"fact","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fækt/","definition":"something that is true and can be checked or proved","arabic":"حقيقة","example":"It is a fact that the phone has a calculator.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"opinion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpɪnjən/","definition":"what someone thinks or feels about something","arabic":"رأي","example":"In my opinion, reading apps are fun.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"distract","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈstrækt/","definition":"to take someone's attention away from what they are doing","arabic":"يشتّت الانتباه","example":"The phone distracted him from his homework.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"survey","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɝˌveɪ/","definition":"a set of questions you ask many people to learn facts or opinions","arabic":"استطلاع","example":"Our class did a survey about screen time.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"parental controls","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈrɛntəl kənˈtroʊlz/","definition":"phone settings that parents use to choose apps and time limits for children","arabic":"أدوات الرقابة الأبوية","example":"Mama set up parental controls on my tablet.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"face to face","pos":"phrase","ipa":"/ˌfeɪs tə ˈfeɪs/","definition":"meeting someone in person, not through a screen","arabic":"وجهًا لوجه","example":"I like talking to my friends face to face.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"prove","pos":"verb","ipa":"/pruv/","definition":"to show that something is true","arabic":"يُثبت","example":"You can prove a fact by checking it.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **fact** is something that is **true** and can be **checked** or **proved** – by counting, measuring, looking it up or observing. *My phone has a calculator.* *15 out of 25 students said…* An **opinion** tells what someone **thinks, feels or believes**. Other people may **disagree**. *A smartphone is the best helper.*\n\n**Opinion signal words:** *I think, I believe, in my opinion, I feel*; **should / must** (*children should wait*); **judgment words** such as *best, worst, great, terrible, enough, too much, fun, boring*. Facts often have **numbers, dates, names** and **things you can observe**.\n\nGood opinion writers **support** their opinions with **facts**. Ask: *What is the writer's opinion? Which facts support it? Is the fact really connected to the opinion?* Then decide which writer you agree with and **why**. Be careful: one sentence can have a **fact and an opinion** together.","examples":["**Fact:** *Doctors say that children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep.* → can be checked.","**Opinion:** *Children should wait until they are older.* → *should* = belief.","**Fact:** *My phone has a dictionary, a calculator and a map.* → you can look.","**Opinion:** *A smartphone is the best helper a student can have.* → *best* = judgment.","**Fact:** *In our class survey, 15 out of 25 students said…* → numbers you can count.","**Opinion:** *A simple phone that only makes calls is enough.* → *enough* = judgment; others may disagree.","**Mixed:** *Most smartphones have parental controls* (fact), *so they are perfect for children* (opinion).","Opinion + supporting fact (Lama): *Phones keep children safe* → *If I miss the bus, I can call my mother.*","Opinion + supporting fact (Faris): *Phones can distract students* → *15 out of 25 said phones stop them from finishing homework.*"],"common_errors":["✗ Thinking that every sentence with a number is true. → ✓ Check it: a fact must be provable. (Check the source.)","✗ Calling an opinion a lie. → ✓ An opinion is not true or false; it is a belief. (Opinions can be different.)","✗ 'Phones are bad' is a fact. → ✓ 'Phones are bad' is an opinion. (*bad* = judgment word.)","✗ Missing the opinion in a mixed sentence. → ✓ Split it: fact part + opinion part. (Read carefully.)","✗ In my opinion, phones has many apps. → ✓ In my opinion, phones have many useful apps. (Plural → **have**.)","✗ Children should to wait. → ✓ Children should wait. (should + base verb (no *to*).)","✗ Agreeing without a reason. → ✓ I agree with Faris because children need nine to twelve hours of sleep. (Support with a fact.)"],"arabic":"الحقيقة شيء صحيح يمكن التحقق منه أو إثباته بالعدّ أو القياس أو البحث أو الملاحظة، أما الرأي فيعبّر عمّا يفكر فيه الشخص أو يشعر به أو يعتقده، وقد يخالفه غيره.\n\nكلمات الرأي: I think وI believe وin my opinion، وكذلك should وmust، وكلمات الحكم مثل best وworst وgreat وenough.\n\nالكاتب الجيد يدعم رأيه بالحقائق؛ فاسأل: ما رأي الكاتب؟ وما الحقائق التي تدعمه؟ وهل ترتبط الحقيقة فعلًا بالرأي؟\n\nانتبه: قد تجمع الجملة الواحدة بين حقيقة ورأي، فافصل بينهما. وبعد should يأتي الفعل دون to."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Fact or opinion? Let's put on our detective glasses!","on_screen":"Fact or Opinion? 🔍","visual":"A cartoon magnifying glass flies over sentences."},{"time":"00:25–01:20","narration":"I believe children aged ten and over should have smartphones…","on_screen":"Yes! – Lama","visual":"Lama at a microphone; icons of a bus, a dictionary and a lock appear."},{"time":"01:20–02:10","narration":"I think children should wait until they are older…","on_screen":"Not Yet! – Faris","visual":"Faris at a microphone; a moon and a clock, a survey chart 15/25, children playing outside."},{"time":"02:10–03:00","narration":"Facts can be checked. Opinions show beliefs: I think, should, best, worst!","on_screen":"FACT ✔ checkable · OPINION 💭 belief","visual":"Sentences slide into a 'Fact' box or an 'Opinion' cloud."},{"time":"03:00–03:30","narration":"Which writer do YOU agree with? Support your opinion with a fact!","on_screen":"My opinion + a fact","visual":"A student raises a hand with a speech bubble."}],"captions_ar":["حقيقة أم رأي؟ لنلبس نظارات المحقق!","أعتقد أن الأطفال من سن العاشرة فما فوق ينبغي أن يمتلكوا هواتف ذكية…","أظن أن على الأطفال أن ينتظروا حتى يكبروا…","الحقائق يمكن التحقق منها، والآراء تعبّر عن المعتقدات: I think وshould وbest وworst!","أيّ الكاتبين توافق؟ ادعم رأيك بحقيقة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly female voice, speed 0.95. Lama and Faris read their parts."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Should Children Have Smartphones?","body":"Our school newspaper asked two Grade 5 students this question. Here are their answers.\n\n**Yes! – by Lama**\n\nI believe children aged ten and over should have smartphones. First, a phone keeps children safe. If I miss the school bus, I can call my mother right away. Second, phones are useful learning tools. My phone has a dictionary, a calculator and a map, and our school uses a reading app for homework. Finally, most smartphones have parental controls that let parents choose which apps their children use and for how long. In my opinion, the problem is not the phone; it is how we use it. With clear family rules, a smartphone is the best helper a student can have.\n\n**Not Yet! – by Faris**\n\nI think children should wait until they are older. Doctors say that children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep every night, but many children keep their phones in bed and stay up late. Phones can also distract students. In our class survey, 15 out of 25 students said that their phones sometimes stop them from finishing their homework. Worst of all, some children meet unkind people online. A simple phone that only makes calls is enough. Children need more time to play, read and talk face to face.","word_count":216,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U6-LS3-1","genre":"opinion/argument","role":"paired","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[{"word":"parental controls","definition":"phone settings that parents use to choose apps and time limits for children","arabic":"أدوات الرقابة الأبوية"},{"word":"distract","definition":"to take someone's attention away from what they are doing","arabic":"يشتّت الانتباه"},{"word":"survey","definition":"a set of questions you ask many people to learn facts or opinions","arabic":"استطلاع"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the two opinions. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ الرأيين واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Who asked Lama and Faris the question about smartphones?","answer":"their school newspaper","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"صحيفة المدرسة.","options":["their teacher","a doctor","their school newspaper","their parents"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What can Lama do if she misses the school bus?","answer":"call her mother right away","explanation":"Lama's text.","explanation_ar":"تتصل بأمها فورًا.","options":["call her mother right away","walk home alone","wait until night","send a letter"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which three tools does Lama's phone have?","answer":"a dictionary, a calculator and a map","explanation":"Lama's text.","explanation_ar":"قاموس وآلة حاسبة وخريطة.","options":["a dictionary, a calculator and a map","a camera, a radio and a TV","a robot, a battery and a clock","a game, a chat and a video"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How many hours of sleep do children aged six to twelve need, according to Faris?","answer":"nine to twelve hours","explanation":"Faris's text.","explanation_ar":"من تسع إلى اثنتي عشرة ساعة.","options":["five to six hours","fifteen hours","three hours","nine to twelve hours"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How many students in Faris's class survey said phones sometimes stop them from finishing homework?","answer":"15 out of 25","explanation":"Faris's text.","explanation_ar":"15 من 25.","options":["25 out of 25","5 out of 15","15 out of 25","none"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What kind of phone does Faris think is enough for children?","answer":"a simple phone that only makes calls","explanation":"Faris's text.","explanation_ar":"هاتف بسيط للمكالمات فقط.","options":["the newest smartphone","a simple phone that only makes calls","a tablet","no phone at all"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Fact or opinion? Choose.","instructions_ar":"حقيقة أم رأي؟ اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which sentence from the smartphone debate is a FACT?","answer":"Our school uses a reading app for homework.","explanation":"Can be checked.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","options":["A smartphone is the best helper a student can have.","Children should wait until they are older.","A simple phone is enough.","Our school uses a reading app for homework."],"dok":1,"skill":"fact and opinion","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence from the smartphone debate is an OPINION?","answer":"Worst of all, some children meet unkind people online.","explanation":"*Worst* = judgment.","explanation_ar":"رأي.","options":["Most smartphones have parental controls.","15 out of 25 students said their phones stop them from finishing homework.","Worst of all, some children meet unkind people online.","My phone has a map."],"dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word in Lama's last sentence shows it is an opinion?","answer":"best","explanation":"Signal word.","explanation_ar":"best.","options":["family","best","student","rules"],"dok":2,"skill":"signal words","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which fact does Faris use to support the idea that phones distract students?","answer":"the class survey: 15 out of 25 students","explanation":"Supporting fact.","explanation_ar":"نتيجة الاستطلاع.","options":["children need nine to twelve hours of sleep","phones have maps","parental controls exist","the class survey: 15 out of 25 students"],"dok":2,"skill":"supporting evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*Most smartphones have parental controls, so they are perfect for children.* What kind of sentence is this?","answer":"a fact and an opinion together","explanation":"Mixed sentence.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة ورأي معًا.","options":["only a fact","only an opinion","a fact and an opinion together","a question"],"dok":3,"skill":"fact and opinion","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about Lama and Faris is TRUE?","answer":"They disagree about the age for a smartphone, but both want children to be safe.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"يختلفان في السن ويتفقان على السلامة.","options":["They disagree about the age for a smartphone, but both want children to be safe.","They agree that every child needs a smartphone.","Neither of them gives any facts.","Faris says phones have no useful apps."],"dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Fact or Opinion Chart.","instructions_ar":"أكمل جدول الحقيقة والرأي.","items":[{"q":"Write one fact and one opinion from Lama's text.","answer":"Fact: My phone has a dictionary, a calculator and a map. Opinion: A smartphone is the best helper a student can have.","explanation":"Lama.","explanation_ar":"لمى.","dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write one fact and one opinion from Faris's text.","answer":"Fact: 15 out of 25 students said their phones sometimes stop them from finishing homework. Opinion: A simple phone that only makes calls is enough.","explanation":"Faris.","explanation_ar":"فارس.","dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion","stage":"freer"},{"q":"List five opinion signal words from the two texts.","answer":"I believe, should, in my opinion, best, I think, worst of all, enough","explanation":"Signal words.","explanation_ar":"كلمات الرأي.","dok":1,"skill":"signal words","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write your opinion about children and smartphones, with one supporting fact from either text.","answer":"I agree with Lama because a phone lets children call their parents if they miss the bus.","explanation":"My opinion.","explanation_ar":"رأيي.","dok":3,"skill":"supporting evidence","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Compare and connect.","instructions_ar":"قارن واربط.","items":[{"q":"Change this opinion into a fact: *Phones are too distracting.*","answer":"In our class survey, 15 out of 25 students said their phones sometimes stop them from finishing homework.","explanation":"Fact.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which writer would agree with Grandpa in *The Phone That Wouldn't Stop*? Explain.","answer":"Faris would agree, because Grandpa also worries about sleep and wants Yousef to charge his phone outside his bedroom.","explanation":"Connect texts.","explanation_ar":"اربط النصين.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"How could Lama use Yousef's story to support her idea that 'the problem is how we use it'?","answer":"She could say that Yousef's phone stopped being a problem when he followed clear rules, such as bedtime mode and the dinner basket.","explanation":"Connect texts.","explanation_ar":"اربط النصين.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one new fact and one new opinion about technology in your school.","answer":"Fact: Our school has a computer lab with 30 computers. Opinion: Coding club is the most exciting club in our school.","explanation":"Create.","explanation_ar":"أنشئ.","dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Should Children Have Smartphones?\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Should Children Have Smartphones?”.","answer":"216 words","title":"Should Children Have Smartphones?","body":"Our school newspaper asked two Grade 5 students this question. Here are their answers.\n\n**Yes! – by Lama**\n\nI believe children aged ten and over should have smartphones. First, a phone keeps children safe. If I miss the school bus, I can call my mother right away. Second, phones are useful learning tools. My phone has a dictionary, a calculator and a map, and our school uses a reading app for homework. Finally, most smartphones have parental controls that let parents choose which apps their children use and for how long. In my opinion, the problem is not the phone; it is how we use it. With clear family rules, a smartphone is the best helper a student can have.\n\n**Not Yet! – by Faris**\n\nI think children should wait until they are older. Doctors say that children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep every night, but many children keep their phones in bed and stay up late. Phones can also distract students. In our class survey, 15 out of 25 students said that their phones sometimes stop them from finishing their homework. Worst of all, some children meet unkind people online. A simple phone that only makes calls is enough. Children need more time to play, read and talk face to face.","words":216,"limit":144}],"time_limit_sec":144},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Fact or Opinion Chart","instructions":"Organizer: Write one fact and one opinion from each writer, the signal words you found and your own opinion with a supporting fact.","items":[{"q":"Lama – a fact","answer":"My phone has a dictionary, a calculator and a map."},{"q":"Lama – an opinion","answer":"A smartphone is the best helper a student can have."},{"q":"Faris – a fact","answer":"15 out of 25 students said their phones sometimes stop them from finishing homework."},{"q":"Faris – an opinion","answer":"A simple phone that only makes calls is enough."},{"q":"Opinion signal words I found","answer":"I believe, should, in my opinion, best, I think, worst of all, enough"},{"q":"My opinion + one supporting fact","answer":"I agree with Faris because children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep."}]}],"game":{"name":"Fact or Opinion Detectives","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Swipe game: a sentence appears on a phone screen; the child swipes left for FACT and right for OPINION, then taps the clue word or checkable detail. Three right answers in a row unlock a detective badge.","Teams take turns drawing a sentence card.","They decide: fact or opinion? They put the card in the right box.","They must name the clue: a signal word or a detail that can be checked.","Mixed sentences must be split into two parts.","Correct sort = 1 point; correct clue = 1 point; mixed sentence split correctly = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *fact*?","answer":"something that is true and can be checked or proved","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","options":["what someone thinks or feels","something that is true and can be checked or proved","a kind of story","a question with no answer"],"dok":1,"skill":"fact and opinion"},{"q":"Which phrase is an opinion signal?","answer":"I believe","explanation":"Signal.","explanation_ar":"أعتقد.","options":["15 out of 25","every night","I believe","in 2025"],"dok":1,"skill":"signal words"},{"q":"*The game **distracted** him, and he forgot his homework.* *Distracted* means ___.","answer":"took his attention away","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"شتّت انتباهه.","options":["helped him focus","made him sleepy","taught him","took his attention away"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which sentence about tablets is a FACT?","answer":"This tablet has a 10-hour battery.","explanation":"Checkable.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","options":["This tablet is the coolest device ever.","Everyone should buy this tablet.","Tablets are boring.","This tablet has a 10-hour battery."],"dok":1,"skill":"fact and opinion"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *should* correctly?","answer":"Children should charge their phones outside the bedroom.","explanation":"should + base verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Children should to charge their phones outside the bedroom.","Children should charging their phones outside the bedroom.","Children should charge their phones outside the bedroom.","Children shoulds charge their phones outside the bedroom."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence about video games is an OPINION?","answer":"Video games are a waste of time.","explanation":"Judgment.","explanation_ar":"رأي.","options":["Video games are a waste of time.","Some video games teach math.","This game has 50 levels.","The game was made in 2024."],"dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion"},{"q":"Why does Lama mention parental controls?","answer":"to show that parents can control children's apps and time, so phones can be safe","explanation":"Supporting fact.","explanation_ar":"لتُظهر أن الأهل يتحكمون بالتطبيقات والوقت.","options":["to show that phones are cheap","to show that parents can control children's apps and time, so phones can be safe","to say that parents don't need rules","to describe a game"],"dok":2,"skill":"supporting evidence"},{"q":"Why do people sometimes disagree about opinions but not about facts?","answer":"Opinions are beliefs, but facts can be checked.","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"لأن الحقائق يمكن التحقق منها.","options":["Opinions are beliefs, but facts can be checked.","Facts are always longer.","Opinions use numbers.","Facts are secrets."],"dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion"},{"q":"*Phones are dangerous, and 3 out of 4 children in our class own one.* Which part is the OPINION?","answer":"Phones are dangerous","explanation":"Mixed sentence.","explanation_ar":"الهواتف خطيرة.","options":["3 out of 4 children","Phones are dangerous","in our class","own one"],"dok":3,"skill":"fact and opinion"},{"q":"Which is the BEST way to make your opinion about phones stronger?","answer":"Support it with a fact that can be checked.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"ادعمه بحقيقة.","options":["Support it with a fact that can be checked.","Repeat it many times.","Write it in capital letters.","Use the word *very* five times."],"dok":3,"skill":"supporting evidence"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fact': a magnifying glass over a checked box"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'opinion': a thought bubble with a heart"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'distract': a student looking away from a book at a phone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'survey': a clipboard with tally marks"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'parental controls': a parent adjusting settings on a child's tablet"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'face to face': two friends talking on a bench"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'prove': a scientist checking a result"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-01","prompt":"A newspaper debate page with two opinions."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-02","prompt":"Fact box and opinion cloud."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS3-03","prompt":"Survey result: 15 out of 25 students."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V01","script":"fact. It is a fact that the phone has a calculator."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V02","script":"opinion. In my opinion, reading apps are fun."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V03","script":"distract. The phone distracted him from his homework."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V04","script":"survey. Our class did a survey about screen time."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V05","script":"parental controls. Mama set up parental controls on my tablet."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V06","script":"face to face. I like talking to my friends face to face."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-V07","script":"prove. You can prove a fact by checking it."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-T1","script":"Our school newspaper asked two Grade 5 students this question. Here are their answers. **Yes! – by Lama** I believe children aged ten and over should have smartphones. First, a phone keeps children safe. If I miss the school bus, I can call my mother right away. Second, phones are useful learning tools. My phone has a dictionary, a calculator and a map, and our school uses a reading app for homework. Finally, most smartphones have parental controls that let parents choose which apps their children use and for how long. In my opinion, the problem is not the phone; it is how we use it. With clear family rules, a smartphone is the best helper a student can have. **Not Yet! – by Faris** I think children should wait until they are older. Doctors say that children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep every night, but many children keep their phones in bed and stay up late. Phones can also distract students. In our class survey, 15 out of 25 students said that their phones sometimes stop them from finishing their homework. Worst of all, some children meet unkind people online. A simple phone that only makes calls is enough. Children need more time to play, read and talk face to face."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS3-VID","script":"Fact or opinion? Let's put on our detective glasses! I believe children aged ten and over should have smartphones… I think children should wait until they are older… Facts can be checked. Opinions show beliefs: I think, should, best, worst! Which writer do YOU agree with? Support your opinion with a fact!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Writing a conclusion","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["write a strong conclusion for an opinion text with a signal phrase","restate my opinion in new words and summarize my reasons in one sentence","end with a final thought: a call to action, a question or a look at the future"],"objectives_ar":["كتابة خاتمة قوية لنص رأي باستخدام عبارة ختامية.","إعادة صياغة رأيي بكلمات جديدة وتلخيص أسبابي في جملة واحدة.","الختام بفكرة أخيرة: دعوة إلى العمل أو سؤال أو نظرة إلى المستقبل."],"success_criteria":["My conclusion starts with a signal phrase such as *In conclusion* or *For these reasons*.","I restate my opinion without copying my first sentence and I sum up my reasons.","I end with a final thought and I do not add new reasons."],"success_criteria_ar":["تبدأ خاتمتي بعبارة ختامية مثل In conclusion أو For these reasons.","أعيد صياغة رأيي دون نسخ جملتي الأولى وألخّص أسبابي.","أختم بفكرة أخيرة ولا أضيف أسبابًا جديدة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"conclusion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈkluʒən/","definition":"the last part of a piece of writing that ends it","arabic":"خاتمة","example":"Write a strong conclusion.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"restate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/riˈsteɪt/","definition":"to say something again in different words","arabic":"يعيد الصياغة / يعيد ذكر","example":"Restate your opinion in the conclusion.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"summarize","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈsʌməˌraɪz/","definition":"to give the main points in a short way","arabic":"يلخّص","example":"Summarize your three reasons in one sentence.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"call to action","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkɔl tu ˈækʃən/","definition":"a sentence that asks the reader to do something","arabic":"دعوة إلى العمل","example":"\"Let's start the club!\" is a call to action.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"coding","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkoʊdɪŋ/","definition":"writing instructions that tell a computer what to do","arabic":"البرمجة","example":"Coding helps us solve problems.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"program","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈproʊˌɡræm/","definition":"a set of instructions for a computer; to write those instructions","arabic":"برنامج / يبرمج","example":"My program moves a robot forward.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"principal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɪnsəpəl/","definition":"the head of a school","arabic":"مدير المدرسة","example":"We sent a letter to the principal.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"The **conclusion** is the **last paragraph** (or the last sentences) of an opinion text. It is the reader's **final memory** of your writing, so it must be **strong**. Start with a **signal phrase**: *In conclusion, To sum up, All in all, For these reasons, That is why…*\n\nA strong conclusion has three parts: (1) **restate your opinion in new words** – don't copy your first sentence; (2) **summarize your reasons** in one short sentence; (3) **end with a final thought**: a **call to action** (*Let's…*), a **question** (*Wouldn't it be great if…?*) or a **look at the future** (*One day…*).\n\n**Don't** add new reasons, say *That's the end* or *I hope you liked my writing*, or weaken your opinion with *But maybe I'm wrong*. **Do** keep the same opinion as your introduction.","examples":["Signal phrase: ***In conclusion,*** / ***To sum up,*** / ***For these reasons,***","Introduction: *I believe our school should start a coding club.* → Restated: ***A coding club would turn us into people who create technology.***","Summary of reasons: ***It would help us think clearly, prepare for the future and make new friends.***","Call to action: ***Let's ask our principal to start the club next term.***","Question: ***Wouldn't it be amazing to build our own school app?***","Look at the future: ***One day, the next great Saudi app might be invented in our classroom!***","Weak ending ✗: *That is the end of my writing.*","New reason ✗: *Also, coding clubs have free snacks.* – a new reason doesn't belong in the conclusion.","Changed opinion ✗: *But maybe we don't need a club.* – keep the same opinion."],"common_errors":["✗ Copying the first sentence exactly. → ✓ Restate it in new words. (Restate, don't repeat.)","✗ Adding a brand-new reason. → ✓ Only sum up the reasons you already gave. (No new reasons.)","✗ That's all. Thank you. → ✓ Let's ask our principal to start the club! (End with a final thought.)","✗ In conclusion coding is useful. → ✓ In conclusion, coding is useful. (Comma after the signal phrase.)","✗ Lets start the club! → ✓ Let's start the club! (Let's = let us (apostrophe).)","✗ To sum up, I think maybe it is a good idea or not. → ✓ To sum up, a coding club is a great idea. (Be clear and confident.)","✗ A conclusion longer than the body. → ✓ Keep it to 2–4 sentences. (Short and strong.)"],"arabic":"الخاتمة هي الفقرة الأخيرة في نص الرأي، وهي آخر ما يتذكره القارئ، فيجب أن تكون قوية، وتبدأ بعبارة ختامية مثل In conclusion أو To sum up أو For these reasons.\n\nللخاتمة القوية ثلاثة أجزاء: إعادة صياغة الرأي بكلمات جديدة، وتلخيص الأسباب في جملة واحدة، والختام بفكرة أخيرة (دعوة إلى العمل أو سؤال أو نظرة إلى المستقبل).\n\nلا تضف أسبابًا جديدة، ولا تقل «هذه نهاية كتابتي»، ولا تُضعف رأيك بعبارة «ربما أكون مخطئًا».\n\nانتبه: ضع فاصلة بعد العبارة الختامية، واكتب Let's بفاصلة عليا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Every great text needs a great ending – just like a great football match!","on_screen":"Writing a Conclusion 🏁","visual":"A ball flies into the net at the last second."},{"time":"00:25–01:20","narration":"Every day, we use apps and games, but very few of us know how they are made…","on_screen":"(model text)","visual":"Rayan in a computer lab; code blocks move a small robot."},{"time":"01:20–02:20","narration":"Signal phrase, restate in new words, sum up the reasons, final thought!","on_screen":"1 Signal · 2 Restate · 3 Sum up · 4 Final thought","visual":"Four steps light up like a progress bar."},{"time":"02:20–03:00","narration":"No new reasons! No 'That's the end'! No 'maybe I'm wrong'!","on_screen":"✗ new reasons · ✗ That's the end · ✗ maybe I'm wrong","visual":"Weak endings are crossed out."},{"time":"03:00–03:30","narration":"End strong – leave your reader thinking or ready to act!","on_screen":"End strong 💪","visual":"A reader closes the text and raises a hand to join the club."}],"captions_ar":["كل نص عظيم يحتاج إلى نهاية عظيمة، تمامًا مثل مباراة كرة قدم رائعة!","كل يوم نستخدم التطبيقات والألعاب، لكن قليلين منا يعرفون كيف تُصنع…","عبارة ختامية، ثم إعادة صياغة الرأي، ثم تلخيص الأسباب، ثم فكرة أخيرة!","لا أسباب جديدة! ولا «هذه هي النهاية»! ولا «ربما أكون مخطئًا»!","اختم بقوة، واترك قارئك يفكر أو مستعدًا للعمل!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, energetic male voice, speed 0.95. Rayan reads his text."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Our School Should Start a Coding Club (by Rayan)","body":"Every day, we use apps and games, but very few of us know how they are made. I believe our school should start a coding club for Grades 4 to 6.\n\nFirst, coding teaches us to solve problems step by step. When my first program did not work, I had to find the mistake and fix it myself. Second, coding is a useful skill for the future, because many jobs use computers. Finally, a club would let students work in teams and share ideas.\n\n**In conclusion,** a coding club would turn us from people who only use technology into people who create it. It would help us think clearly, prepare for the future and make new friends. Let's ask our principal to start the club next term. Who knows? The next great Saudi app might be invented in our classroom!","word_count":140,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U6-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Signal phrase:** *In conclusion,* tells the reader that the text is ending.","**Restate the opinion in NEW words:** *should start a coding club* → *would turn us … into people who create it*.","**Summarize the reasons in ONE sentence:** *think clearly, prepare for the future and make new friends*.","**Final thought – a call to action:** *Let's ask our principal to start the club next term.*","**Final thought – a look at the future:** *The next great Saudi app might be invented in our classroom!*","**No new reasons:** the conclusion does not add a fourth reason."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Rayan's text. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ نص ريان واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What is Rayan's opinion?","answer":"Our school should start a coding club.","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"يجب أن تبدأ المدرسة ناديًا للبرمجة.","options":["Coding is too difficult for children.","Our school should start a coding club.","Students should not use apps.","The principal should buy new computers."],"dok":1,"skill":"opinion","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which signal phrase starts Rayan's conclusion?","answer":"In conclusion,","explanation":"Signal phrase.","explanation_ar":"In conclusion.","options":["In conclusion,","First,","Second,","Every day,"],"dok":1,"skill":"signal phrases","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which reasons does Rayan sum up in his conclusion?","answer":"think clearly, prepare for the future and make new friends","explanation":"Summary.","explanation_ar":"التفكير بوضوح والاستعداد للمستقبل وتكوين أصدقاء.","options":["free snacks, games and holidays","faster Wi-Fi and new tablets","think clearly, prepare for the future and make new friends","homework, tests and grades"],"dok":1,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which sentence is Rayan's call to action?","answer":"Let's ask our principal to start the club next term.","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"دعوة إلى العمل.","options":["Every day, we use apps and games.","Let's ask our principal to start the club next term.","Second, coding is a useful skill.","Coding teaches us to solve problems."],"dok":1,"skill":"final thoughts","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What happened when Rayan's first program did not work?","answer":"He found the mistake and fixed it himself.","explanation":"Body paragraph.","explanation_ar":"وجد الخطأ وأصلحه.","options":["He gave up.","His teacher fixed it.","He found the mistake and fixed it himself.","He bought a new computer."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Does Rayan add a NEW reason in his conclusion?","answer":"No – he only sums up the reasons he already gave.","explanation":"No new reasons.","explanation_ar":"لا.","options":["Yes – he adds free snacks.","Yes – he adds new computers.","Yes – he adds trips.","No – he only sums up the reasons he already gave."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Strong or weak? Choose.","instructions_ar":"قوية أم ضعيفة؟ اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the STRONGEST conclusion for a text saying *Families should have a device-free dinner*?","answer":"To sum up, phone-free dinners help families talk, listen and enjoy their food. Let's try one tonight!","explanation":"Strong.","explanation_ar":"الأقوى.","options":["To sum up, phone-free dinners help families talk, listen and enjoy their food. Let's try one tonight!","That's the end of my text.","Also, phones can break if you drop them in the soup.","But maybe phones at dinner are fine."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which conclusion about robots in hospitals RESTATES the opinion in new words?","answer":"Robots can be a doctor's best helper.","explanation":"Restate.","explanation_ar":"إعادة صياغة.","options":["I believe hospitals should use robots.","Robots are made of metal.","Hospitals have many rooms.","Robots can be a doctor's best helper."],"dok":2,"skill":"restating","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence does NOT belong in a conclusion about a school reading app?","answer":"Also, the app has a new dark mode.","explanation":"New detail.","explanation_ar":"تفصيلة جديدة.","options":["In conclusion, the reading app helps us read more.","Let's ask every class to use it.","One day, our whole school might love reading.","Also, the app has a new dark mode."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which final thought about online safety is a QUESTION?","answer":"Wouldn't it be great if every child knew how to stay safe online?","explanation":"Question.","explanation_ar":"سؤال.","options":["Let's make a safety poster.","Wouldn't it be great if every child knew how to stay safe online?","One day, the internet will be safer.","Online safety is important."],"dok":2,"skill":"final thoughts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the coding club uses *Let's* correctly?","answer":"Let's start a coding club next term!","explanation":"Let's + base verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Lets start a coding club next term!","Let's to start a coding club next term!","Let's start a coding club next term!","Let's starting a coding club next term!"],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which ending WEAKENS an opinion that tablets help students learn?","answer":"But maybe tablets are not useful at all.","explanation":"Weak.","explanation_ar":"يُضعف الرأي.","options":["But maybe tablets are not useful at all.","For these reasons, tablets are excellent learning tools.","Let's use tablets wisely in every class.","One day, every student might have a digital library."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the conclusion with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل الخاتمة من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"________ conclusion, video calls help families stay close.","answer":"In","explanation":"Signal phrase.","explanation_ar":"في الختام.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"To ________ up, robots can do dangerous jobs and keep workers safe.","answer":"sum","explanation":"Signal phrase.","explanation_ar":"باختصار.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"For these ________, our class should have a tablet corner.","answer":"reasons","explanation":"Signal phrase.","explanation_ar":"لهذه الأسباب.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________ ask our teacher to start a robotics club!","answer":"Let's","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"لنطلب.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________ day, students might learn with robots in every classroom.","answer":"One","explanation":"Look at the future.","explanation_ar":"يومًا ما.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________ it be great if every family had a screen-free hour?","answer":"Wouldn't","explanation":"Question.","explanation_ar":"أليس رائعًا.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write and fix conclusions.","instructions_ar":"اكتب الخواتيم وحسّنها.","items":[{"q":"Restate this opinion in new words: *I believe students should learn to code.*","answer":"Learning to code would help every student think and create.","explanation":"Restate.","explanation_ar":"أعد الصياغة.","dok":2,"skill":"restating","stage":"production"},{"q":"Summarize these three reasons in ONE sentence: *robots do dangerous jobs; robots work all day without getting tired; robots help doctors in operations.*","answer":"Robots keep workers safe, never get tired and help doctors.","explanation":"Summarize.","explanation_ar":"لخّص.","dok":2,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Fix this weak conclusion: *That's all about online safety. Bye.*","answer":"In conclusion, staying safe online protects our privacy and our feelings. Let's always think before we click!","explanation":"Revise.","explanation_ar":"حسّن.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a full conclusion for: *Our school should have a technology fair.*","answer":"For these reasons, a technology fair would let us share our inventions, learn from each other and inspire younger students. Let's ask our principal to hold one this spring. Who knows what we might invent?","explanation":"Write.","explanation_ar":"اكتب.","dok":3,"skill":"writing conclusions","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Conclusion Builder Relay","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Build a conclusion: the child drags four cards into order (signal → restate → sum up → final thought) and throws trap cards into the recycle bin. A strong conclusion makes a rocket launch.","Each team gets a mixed set of cards for one opinion.","Runners take one card at a time to the board and build the conclusion in order.","Trap cards must go in the bin.","The team reads its conclusion aloud.","Correct order = 3 points; every trap card binned = 1 point; best final thought = 2 bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *conclusion*?","answer":"the last part of a piece of writing that ends it","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","options":["the title","the first sentence","a list of new reasons","the last part of a piece of writing that ends it"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which is a signal phrase for a conclusion?","answer":"To sum up,","explanation":"Signal phrase.","explanation_ar":"To sum up.","options":["To sum up,","First,","Once upon a time,","Next,"],"dok":1,"skill":"signal phrases"},{"q":"*Please **restate** your opinion at the end.* *Restate* means ___.","answer":"say it again in different words","explanation":"Restate.","explanation_ar":"أعد صياغته.","options":["forget it","change it to the opposite","copy it exactly","say it again in different words"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which is a *call to action*?","answer":"Let's turn off our screens for one hour tonight!","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"دعوة إلى العمل.","options":["Screens are bright.","I have a tablet.","Let's turn off our screens for one hour tonight!","Phones were invented long ago."],"dok":1,"skill":"final thoughts"},{"q":"Which sentence about video calls is punctuated correctly?","answer":"In conclusion, video calls keep families close.","explanation":"Comma after the signal phrase.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["In conclusion, video calls keep families close.","In conclusion video calls keep families close.","In, conclusion video calls keep families close.","In conclusion video calls, keep families close."],"dok":2,"skill":"punctuation"},{"q":"What should a conclusion NOT include?","answer":"a brand-new reason","explanation":"No new reasons.","explanation_ar":"سبب جديد.","options":["a restated opinion","a brand-new reason","a summary of the reasons","a final thought"],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions"},{"q":"Which final thought about robots LOOKS AT THE FUTURE?","answer":"One day, robots might help every family at home.","explanation":"Future.","explanation_ar":"نظرة إلى المستقبل.","options":["Let's build a robot.","One day, robots might help every family at home.","Do you like robots?","Robots are metal."],"dok":2,"skill":"final thoughts"},{"q":"A writer's introduction says *Tablets help us learn*. Which conclusion KEEPS the same opinion?","answer":"For these reasons, tablets are powerful learning tools.","explanation":"Same opinion.","explanation_ar":"يحافظ على الرأي نفسه.","options":["For these reasons, tablets are powerful learning tools.","In the end, tablets are useless.","But maybe books are better than tablets.","That's the end."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions"},{"q":"Why is the conclusion so important in an opinion text?","answer":"It is the reader's final memory, so it can persuade the reader to agree or act.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"آخر ما يتذكره القارئ.","options":["It is the longest paragraph.","It gives the title.","It is the reader's final memory, so it can persuade the reader to agree or act.","It lists new facts."],"dok":3,"skill":"purpose"},{"q":"Which conclusion about a robotics club is the STRONGEST?","answer":"In conclusion, a robotics club would help us invent, cooperate and dream big. Let's start one this year – our first robot is waiting!","explanation":"Strongest.","explanation_ar":"الأقوى.","options":["Robotics is fun. The end.","In conclusion, a robotics club would help us invent, cooperate and dream big. Let's start one this year – our first robot is waiting!","Also, robots can dance.","Maybe a club is a good idea, or maybe not."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'conclusion': the last puzzle piece in a puzzle"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'restate': two speech bubbles with the same idea"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'summarize': three cards folded into one"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'call to action': a hand pointing forward with an arrow"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'coding': colorful code blocks on a screen"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'program': a small robot following arrows"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'principal': a school principal at a desk"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-01","prompt":"Students coding a robot in a school lab."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-02","prompt":"Four parts of a strong conclusion."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS4-03","prompt":"A weak ending and a strong ending."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V01","script":"conclusion. Write a strong conclusion."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V02","script":"restate. Restate your opinion in the conclusion."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V03","script":"summarize. Summarize your three reasons in one sentence."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V04","script":"call to action. \"Let's start the club!\" is a call to action."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V05","script":"coding. Coding helps us solve problems."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V06","script":"program. My program moves a robot forward."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-V07","script":"principal. We sent a letter to the principal."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-T1","script":"Every day, we use apps and games, but very few of us know how they are made. I believe our school should start a coding club for Grades 4 to 6. First, coding teaches us to solve problems step by step. When my first program did not work, I had to find the mistake and fix it myself. Second, coding is a useful skill for the future, because many jobs use computers. Finally, a club would let students work in teams and share ideas. **In conclusion,** a coding club would turn us from people who only use technology into people who create it. It would help us think clearly, prepare for the future and make new friends. Let's ask our principal to start the club next term. Who knows? The next great Saudi app might be invented in our classroom!"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS4-VID","script":"Every great text needs a great ending – just like a great football match! Every day, we use apps and games, but very few of us know how they are made… Signal phrase, restate in new words, sum up the reasons, final thought! No new reasons! No 'That's the end'! No 'maybe I'm wrong'! End strong – leave your reader thinking or ready to act!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: Screen-Time Rules for My Family","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a two-paragraph opinion text about screen-time rules for my family with the Opinion Planner","write 120–150 words: a hook, a clear opinion, three rules with reasons and facts, and a strong conclusion","revise, edit and publish my rules as a family screen-time poster"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لنص رأي من فقرتين عن قواعد وقت الشاشة لأسرتي.","كتابة 120–150 كلمة: جملة جذب ورأي واضح وثلاث قواعد بأسباب وحقائق وخاتمة قوية.","تحسين النص وتدقيقه ونشر القواعد في ملصق لوقت الشاشة في الأسرة."],"success_criteria":["Paragraph 1 has a hook, a clear opinion and two rules, each with a reason.","I support my rules with facts and keep facts and opinions clear.","Paragraph 2 has the third rule and a conclusion with a signal phrase, a restated opinion and a call to action (120–150 words)."],"success_criteria_ar":["الفقرة الأولى فيها جملة جذب ورأي واضح وقاعدتان لكل منهما سبب.","أدعم قواعدي بالحقائق وأميّز بين الحقائق والآراء.","الفقرة الثانية فيها القاعدة الثالثة وخاتمة بعبارة ختامية وإعادة صياغة الرأي ودعوة إلى العمل (120–150 كلمة)."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"screen time","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈskrin ˌtaɪm/","definition":"the time you spend looking at a phone, a tablet, a computer or a TV","arabic":"وقت الشاشة","example":"We want to cut our screen time.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"rule","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rul/","definition":"something that tells you what you must or must not do","arabic":"قاعدة","example":"Our first rule is no phones at dinner.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"average","pos":"adjective / noun","ipa":"/ˈævərɪdʒ/","definition":"usual or typical; the number you get when you share a total equally","arabic":"متوسط / معدّل","example":"My average screen time is four hours a day.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"limit","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈlɪmɪt/","definition":"the largest amount that is allowed; to keep something under a certain amount","arabic":"حدّ / يحدّ من","example":"Huda has a limit of one hour a day.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"advise","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ədˈvaɪz/","definition":"to tell someone what you think they should do","arabic":"ينصح","example":"Doctors advise children to sleep nine to twelve hours.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"habit","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhæbɪt/","definition":"something you do often, often without thinking","arabic":"عادة","example":"Charging my phone in the kitchen is a good habit.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"persuade","pos":"verb","ipa":"/pərˈsweɪd/","definition":"to make someone agree to do or believe something","arabic":"يُقنع","example":"I want to persuade my family to try the rules.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **opinion text** tries to **persuade** the reader. It gives a **clear opinion**, **reasons** and **facts** that support them, and a **strong conclusion**. Use opinion words (*I believe, should, In my opinion*) for your opinion, and **checkable facts** (numbers, expert advice) for your support.\n\n**Paragraph 1:** a **hook** (a question or a surprising fact) + your **opinion** + **Rule 1** with a reason/fact + **Rule 2** with a reason/fact. **Paragraph 2:** **Rule 3** with a reason/fact + a **conclusion**: signal phrase, restate your opinion and sum up the reasons, then a **call to action**.\n\nUse **linking words**: *First, Second, Finally, because, so, In conclusion*. Use **should + base verb** for rules (*screens should go off*). Keep it **120–150 words**.","examples":["Hook (question): ***How many hours does your family spend looking at screens?***","Hook (surprising fact): ***Last week, my family spent sixteen hours a day on screens altogether!***","Opinion: ***I believe our family needs three clear screen-time rules.***","Rule + fact: ***First, there should be no phones at dinner, because screens were on during four of our seven dinners and we hardly talked.***","Rule + expert fact: ***Second, all screens should go off one hour before bed. Sleep experts advise this, and children my age need nine to twelve hours of sleep.***","Rule + reason: ***Finally, we should finish homework before games, so school always comes first.***","Conclusion – sum up: ***In conclusion, these rules would help us talk more, sleep better and do well at school.***","Conclusion – restate: ***Technology is a great servant, but our family should be in charge.***","Call to action: ***Let's write the rules on the fridge and start this weekend!***"],"common_errors":["✗ Rules with no reasons: No phones. No games. → ✓ No phones at dinner, because we hardly talked. (Rule + reason.)","✗ Using opinions as facts: Games are the worst thing. → ✓ Use a fact: I didn't finish my homework twice because of a game. (Support with facts.)","✗ Screens should to go off at nine. → ✓ Screens should go off at nine. (should + base verb.)","✗ Rules only for other people: Yasser must stop playing. → ✓ Our family should… / We should… (Rules for everyone, including you.)","✗ Too many rules (ten rules). → ✓ Choose the three most important rules. (Three strong rules.)","✗ Ending: That's my rules. → ✓ Let's write the rules on the fridge and start this weekend! (Call to action.)","✗ Writing 220 words. → ✓ Keep it 120–150 words. (Check the length.)"],"arabic":"نص الرأي يحاول إقناع القارئ؛ فهو يقدّم رأيًا واضحًا وأسبابًا وحقائق تدعمه وخاتمة قوية. استخدم كلمات الرأي للرأي، والحقائق القابلة للتحقق للدعم.\n\nالفقرة الأولى: جملة جذب ورأيك والقاعدتان الأولى والثانية مع سبب أو حقيقة لكل منهما. الفقرة الثانية: القاعدة الثالثة مع سبب أو حقيقة، ثم خاتمة بعبارة ختامية وإعادة صياغة الرأي وتلخيص الأسباب ودعوة إلى العمل.\n\nاستخدم كلمات الربط: First, Second, Finally, because, so, In conclusion، واستخدم should مع الفعل المجرد للقواعد.\n\nاجعل القواعد للأسرة كلها، وأنت منها، والتزم بطول 120–150 كلمة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Sixteen hours a day on screens? Time for some family rules!","on_screen":"Screen-Time Rules for My Family 🏠📱","visual":"A clock spins over a family each looking at a different screen."},{"time":"00:25–01:05","narration":"Here is the Al-Harbi family's screen-time diary. What do you notice?","on_screen":"16 hours a day · 4 of 7 dinners · bed after 11 p.m.","visual":"Numbers pop out of a diary page."},{"time":"01:05–02:05","narration":"How many hours does your family spend looking at screens?…","on_screen":"(model text)","visual":"A family dinner with a phone basket; screens switching off at bedtime; homework before a game."},{"time":"02:05–02:55","narration":"Paragraph 1: hook, opinion, Rule 1 and Rule 2. Paragraph 2: Rule 3 and a strong conclusion!","on_screen":"P1 · P2","visual":"Two blocks build with icons and arrows."},{"time":"02:55–03:30","narration":"Support your rules with facts and persuade your family. Then publish your poster!","on_screen":"Facts persuade ✔","visual":"A colorful family poster appears on a fridge."}],"captions_ar":["ست عشرة ساعة يوميًا أمام الشاشات؟ حان وقت قواعد الأسرة!","هذا سجل وقت الشاشة لأسرة الحربي. ماذا تلاحظ؟","كم ساعة تقضي أسرتك أمام الشاشات؟…","الفقرة الأولى: جذب ورأي والقاعدتان الأولى والثانية. الفقرة الثانية: القاعدة الثالثة وخاتمة قوية!","ادعم قواعدك بالحقائق وأقنع أسرتك، ثم انشر ملصقك!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly female voice, speed 0.95. A student reads the model text."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"The Al-Harbi Family Screen-Time Diary (one school week)","body":"**Average screen time each day:** Baba – 3 hours (work emails, news) · Mama – 2 hours (recipes, calls with relatives) · Yasser, 13 – 5 hours (games, videos) · Me, 11 – 4 hours (games, group chat, 30 minutes on a reading app) · Huda, 5 – 2 hours (cartoons) · **Total: 16 hours a day**\n\n**Notes:** Screens were on during 4 of our 7 dinners. Yasser went to bed after 11 p.m. on three school nights. I didn't finish my homework twice because I was playing a game. Huda cried when the tablet was taken away.\n\n**Fact box:** Doctors say that children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep a night. Many sleep experts advise turning off screens at least 30 minutes to an hour before bed. For children aged two to five, many doctors advise no more than about one hour of screen time a day.\n\n**Rule ideas:** No phones at dinner · Screens off one hour before bed · Devices charge in the kitchen at night · Homework before games · A screen-free family afternoon every Friday · Ask a parent before downloading a new app","word_count":180,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U6-LS5-1","genre":"data notes","role":"model","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **three rules** that would help your family most.","Support each rule with a **reason** and, if you can, a **fact** from the diary or the fact box.","Plan **two paragraphs**: (1) hook, opinion and two rules with reasons; (2) the third rule with a reason and a **conclusion** (signal phrase, restate and sum up, call to action)."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the screen-time diary. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ سجل وقت الشاشة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Who in the Al-Harbi family has the MOST screen time each day?","answer":"Yasser – 5 hours","explanation":"Diary.","explanation_ar":"ياسر.","options":["Baba – 3 hours","Mama – 2 hours","Yasser – 5 hours","Huda – 2 hours"],"dok":1,"skill":"reading data","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How many hours of screen time does the Al-Harbi family have altogether each day?","answer":"16 hours","explanation":"Total.","explanation_ar":"16 ساعة.","options":["5 hours","16 hours","10 hours","24 hours"],"dok":1,"skill":"reading data","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"During how many of the family's dinners were screens on?","answer":"4 of 7","explanation":"Notes.","explanation_ar":"4 من 7.","options":["4 of 7","7 of 7","1 of 7","none"],"dok":1,"skill":"reading data","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How many times did the writer not finish homework because of a game?","answer":"twice","explanation":"Notes.","explanation_ar":"مرتين.","options":["once","five times","never","twice"],"dok":1,"skill":"reading data","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What do many sleep experts advise about screens and bedtime?","answer":"Turn them off at least 30 minutes to an hour before bed.","explanation":"Fact box.","explanation_ar":"إطفاؤها قبل النوم بنصف ساعة إلى ساعة.","options":["Use them in bed until you fall asleep.","Turn them off at midnight.","Keep them on all night.","Turn them off at least 30 minutes to an hour before bed."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Huda is five years old. Why might the family limit her screen time?","answer":"Many doctors advise about one hour a day for children aged two to five, but she has two.","explanation":"Fact box + diary.","explanation_ar":"لأن الأطباء ينصحون بساعة تقريبًا وهي تقضي ساعتين.","options":["She doesn't like cartoons.","The tablet is broken.","Many doctors advise about one hour a day for children aged two to five, but she has two.","She has homework."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your opinion text. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطط لنص الرأي: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST hook for a text about family screen-time rules?","answer":"Did you know that my family spent sixteen hours a day on screens last week?","explanation":"Hook.","explanation_ar":"الأفضل.","options":["Did you know that my family spent sixteen hours a day on screens last week?","This is my text about screens.","Screens.","I have a phone and a tablet and a TV."],"dok":1,"skill":"hooks","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is a clear OPINION for a screen-time text?","answer":"I believe our family should have a screen-free afternoon every Friday.","explanation":"Opinion.","explanation_ar":"رأي واضح.","options":["Baba uses screens for three hours.","I believe our family should have a screen-free afternoon every Friday.","Huda watches cartoons.","We have five devices."],"dok":2,"skill":"opinion","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which FACT best supports the rule *Devices charge in the kitchen at night*?","answer":"Yasser went to bed after 11 p.m. on three school nights.","explanation":"Supporting fact.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة داعمة.","options":["Mama uses screens for recipes.","Huda likes cartoons.","Baba reads the news.","Yasser went to bed after 11 p.m. on three school nights."],"dok":2,"skill":"supporting evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which family rule is written correctly with *should*?","answer":"We should ask a parent before downloading a new app.","explanation":"should + base verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We should to ask a parent before downloading a new app.","We should ask a parent before downloading a new app.","We should asking a parent before downloading a new app.","We shoulds ask a parent before downloading a new app."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which linking word fits? *Homework should come before games, ___ we don't forget school work.*","answer":"so","explanation":"Result.","explanation_ar":"so.","options":["so","but","although","or"],"dok":2,"skill":"linking words","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which conclusion for a family screen-time text is the STRONGEST?","answer":"In conclusion, these rules would help us sleep better and talk more. Let's start them tonight!","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الأقوى.","options":["Also, we should buy a new TV.","That's my rules. Bye.","In conclusion, these rules would help us sleep better and talk more. Let's start them tonight!","But maybe screens are fine all day."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your text with the Opinion Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط لنصك بمخطط الرأي.","items":[{"q":"Hook and opinion.","answer":"Hook: How many hours does your family spend looking at screens? Opinion: Our family needs three clear screen-time rules.","explanation":"Hook + opinion.","explanation_ar":"الجذب والرأي.","dok":1,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Rule 1 and Rule 2, each with a reason or a fact.","answer":"1: no phones at dinner – screens on during 4 of 7 dinners. 2: screens off one hour before bed – experts advise it; children need 9–12 hours of sleep.","explanation":"Rules 1–2.","explanation_ar":"القاعدتان 1–2.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Rule 3 with a reason or a fact.","answer":"homework before games – I didn't finish my homework twice because of a game","explanation":"Rule 3.","explanation_ar":"القاعدة 3.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Conclusion notes: signal phrase, restate and sum up, call to action.","answer":"In conclusion – talk more, sleep better, do well at school – our family in charge – Let's write the rules on the fridge!","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and publish your opinion text.","instructions_ar":"اكتب نص رأيك وحسّنه ودققه وانشره.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1: hook, opinion, Rule 1 and Rule 2 with reasons or facts.","answer":"How many hours does your family spend looking at screens? Last week, my family spent sixteen hours a day on screens! I believe we need three clear rules. First, there should be no phones at dinner, because screens were on during four of our seven dinners. Second, all screens should go off one hour before bed, because children my age need nine to twelve hours of sleep.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الأولى.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 2: Rule 3 and your conclusion.","answer":"Finally, we should finish homework before games, so school always comes first. In conclusion, these rules would help us talk more, sleep better and do well at school. Let's write them on the fridge and start this weekend!","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثانية.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence that answers an objection, e.g., *But I need my tablet for homework.*","answer":"Of course, we can still use the reading app for homework, but games and videos should wait until our work is done.","explanation":"Objection.","explanation_ar":"الرد على اعتراض.","dok":3,"skill":"reasoning","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence you improved by adding a fact (before → after).","answer":"Before: Screens are bad at night. After: Screens should go off one hour before bed, because children my age need nine to twelve hours of sleep.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Opinion Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Choose three rules. Write a reason and a fact for each, then plan your conclusion.","items":[{"q":"Hook (question or surprising fact)","answer":"How many hours does your family spend looking at screens?"},{"q":"My opinion","answer":"Our family needs three clear screen-time rules."},{"q":"Rule 1 + reason/fact","answer":"no phones at dinner – screens on during 4 of 7 dinners; we hardly talked"},{"q":"Rule 2 + reason/fact","answer":"screens off one hour before bed – experts advise it; children need 9–12 hours of sleep"},{"q":"Rule 3 + reason/fact","answer":"homework before games – so school comes first"},{"q":"Conclusion (signal phrase, restate and sum up, call to action)","answer":"In conclusion – talk more, sleep better, do well at school – our family in charge – Let's write the rules on the fridge!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U6-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Write a two-paragraph opinion text (120–150 words) called *Screen-Time Rules for My Family*. Paragraph 1: a hook, your opinion and two rules, each with a reason or a fact. Paragraph 2: a third rule with a reason or a fact and a strong conclusion (signal phrase, restated opinion and summary, call to action). Write a two-paragraph opinion text (120–150 words) called *Screen-Time Rules for My Family*. Paragraph 1: a hook, your opinion and two rules, each with a reason or a fact. Paragraph 2: a third rule with a reason or a fact and a strong conclusion (signal phrase, restated opinion and summary, call to action).","instructions_ar":"اكتب نص رأي من فقرتين (120–150 كلمة) بعنوان «قواعد وقت الشاشة لأسرتي». الفقرة الأولى: جملة جذب ورأيك وقاعدتان مع سبب أو حقيقة لكل منهما. الفقرة الثانية: قاعدة ثالثة مع سبب أو حقيقة وخاتمة قوية (عبارة ختامية وإعادة صياغة الرأي وتلخيص ودعوة إلى العمل).","prompt":"Write a two-paragraph opinion text (120–150 words) called *Screen-Time Rules for My Family*. Paragraph 1: a hook, your opinion and two rules, each with a reason or a fact. Paragraph 2: a third rule with a reason or a fact and a strong conclusion (signal phrase, restated opinion and summary, call to action).","checklist":["My hook is a question or a surprising fact.","My opinion is clear (I believe… / should…).","I wrote three rules with should + base verb.","Each rule has a reason, and at least two have a fact.","I used First, Second, Finally, because and so.","My conclusion has a signal phrase, a restated opinion and a summary of my reasons.","I ended with a call to action and added no new reasons.","My text has two paragraphs and 120–150 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":120,"max_words":170,"organizer":"Opinion Planner (hook – opinion – Rule 1 – Rule 2 – Rule 3 – conclusion)","items":[{"q":"Write a two-paragraph opinion text (120–150 words) called *Screen-Time Rules for My Family*. Paragraph 1: a hook, your opinion and two rules, each with a reason or a fact. Paragraph 2: a third rule with a reason or a fact and a strong conclusion (signal phrase, restated opinion and summary, call to action).","answer":"How many hours does your family spend looking at screens? Last week, my family spent sixteen hours a day on screens altogether! I believe our family needs three clear screen-time rules. First, there should be no phones at dinner, because screens were on during four of our seven dinners and we hardly talked. Second, all screens should go off one hour before bed. Sleep experts advise this, and children my age need nine to twelve hours of sleep. (new paragraph) Finally, we should finish our homework before games, so school always comes first. In conclusion, these rules would help us talk more, sleep better and do well at school. Technology is a great servant, but our family should be in charge. Let's write the rules on the fridge and start this weekend!","_w":{"checklist":["My hook is a question or a surprising fact.","My opinion is clear (I believe… / should…).","I wrote three rules with should + base verb.","Each rule has a reason, and at least two have a fact.","I used First, Second, Finally, because and so.","My conclusion has a signal phrase, a restated opinion and a summary of my reasons.","I ended with a call to action and added no new reasons.","My text has two paragraphs and 120–150 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"min":120,"max":170}}]}],"game":{"name":"Persuade the Parents","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Debate simulator: the child chooses a rule, then picks the best reason and fact from three options each; the parent characters' faces change from doubtful to smiling. Three approved rules unlock a family poster.","Three students act as the parent panel.","Each team chooses a rule card and plans a 30-second speech: opinion + reason + fact + call to action.","The panel can ask one question; the team must answer politely.","The panel stamps 'APPROVED' if the speech has a reason AND a fact.","Approved rule = 3 points; best call to action = 1 bonus point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is the purpose of a family screen-time opinion text?","answer":"to persuade the family to agree with the rules and try them","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"الإقناع.","options":["to tell a made-up story","to describe a picture","to give a recipe","to persuade the family to agree with the rules and try them"],"dok":1,"skill":"genre"},{"q":"What does *screen time* mean?","answer":"the time you spend looking at phones, tablets, computers or TVs","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"وقت الشاشة.","options":["the time a movie starts","the size of a screen","a kind of clock","the time you spend looking at phones, tablets, computers or TVs"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Doctors **advise** children to sleep nine to twelve hours.* *Advise* means ___.","answer":"tell someone what they think they should do","explanation":"Advise.","explanation_ar":"ينصح.","options":["forbid","tell someone what they think they should do","forget","ask a question"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What belongs in Paragraph 2 of your screen-time text?","answer":"Rule 3 with a reason and the conclusion","explanation":"Organization.","explanation_ar":"القاعدة الثالثة والخاتمة.","options":["only the hook","the title and your name","a list of all your games","Rule 3 with a reason and the conclusion"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization"},{"q":"Which sentence about the tablet rule is correct?","answer":"The tablet should stay in the kitchen at night.","explanation":"should + base verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The tablet should stays in the kitchen at night.","The tablet should stay in the kitchen at night.","The tablet should to stay in the kitchen at night.","The tablet should staying in the kitchen at night."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which is a FACT that could support a screen-time rule?","answer":"Children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep.","explanation":"Fact.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","options":["Screens are the worst invention.","Games are boring.","Children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep.","My family should be happier."],"dok":2,"skill":"fact and opinion"},{"q":"Why should rules in your text include YOU too, not only your brother?","answer":"Rules for everyone are fairer and more persuasive.","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"لأنها أعدل وأكثر إقناعًا.","options":["Because brothers never use screens.","Because the text must be longer.","Rules for everyone are fairer and more persuasive.","Because rules are only for adults."],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasion"},{"q":"Which linking word introduces your LAST rule?","answer":"Finally,","explanation":"Linking word.","explanation_ar":"Finally.","options":["Finally,","First,","Once upon a time,","Then suddenly,"],"dok":2,"skill":"linking words"},{"q":"A partner's text has eight rules and no facts. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Choose the three most important rules and support each with a reason or a fact.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"اختر ثلاث قواعد وادعمها.","options":["Add more rules.","Remove the conclusion.","Choose the three most important rules and support each with a reason or a fact.","Write everything in capital letters."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"},{"q":"Which call to action would MOST persuade a family to try the rules?","answer":"Let's try the rules for one week and see how we feel!","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"الأكثر إقناعًا.","options":["Let's try the rules for one week and see how we feel!","Rules are rules.","That's all.","You must obey me now."],"dok":3,"skill":"persuasion"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'screen time': a clock on a tablet screen"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rule': a list of rules on a fridge"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'average': a simple bar chart with a line across"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'limit': a timer showing one hour"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'advise': a doctor talking to a family"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'habit': a phone charging on a kitchen counter"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'persuade': a child explaining a poster to parents"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-01","prompt":"A family screen-time rules poster."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-02","prompt":"A family screen-time diary."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U6-LS5-03","prompt":"A phone-shaped opinion planner."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V01","script":"screen time. We want to cut our screen time."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V02","script":"rule. Our first rule is no phones at dinner."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V03","script":"average. My average screen time is four hours a day."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V04","script":"limit. Huda has a limit of one hour a day."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V05","script":"advise. Doctors advise children to sleep nine to twelve hours."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V06","script":"habit. Charging my phone in the kitchen is a good habit."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-V07","script":"persuade. I want to persuade my family to try the rules."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-T1","script":"**Average screen time each day:** Baba – 3 hours (work emails, news) · Mama – 2 hours (recipes, calls with relatives) · Yasser, 13 – 5 hours (games, videos) · Me, 11 – 4 hours (games, group chat, 30 minutes on a reading app) · Huda, 5 – 2 hours (cartoons) · **Total: 16 hours a day** **Notes:** Screens were on during 4 of our 7 dinners. Yasser went to bed after 11 p.m. on three school nights. I didn't finish my homework twice because I was playing a game. Huda cried when the tablet was taken away. **Fact box:** Doctors say that children aged six to twelve need nine to twelve hours of sleep a night. Many sleep experts advise turning off screens at least 30 minutes to an hour before bed. For children aged two to five, many doctors advise no more than about one hour of screen time a day. **Rule ideas:** No phones at dinner · Screens off one hour before bed · Devices charge in the kitchen at night · Homework before games · A screen-free family afternoon every Friday · Ask a parent before downloading a new app"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U6-LS5-VID","script":"Sixteen hours a day on screens? Time for some family rules! Here is the Al-Harbi family's screen-time diary. What do you notice? How many hours does your family spend looking at screens?… Paragraph 1: hook, opinion, Rule 1 and Rule 2. Paragraph 2: Rule 3 and a strong conclusion! Support your rules with facts and persuade your family. Then publish your poster!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"The ___ on Grandpa's watch is so small that he can hardly read his messages.","answer":"screen","explanation":"Screen.","explanation_ar":"الشاشة.","options":["screen","battery","robot","password"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"A smartwatch, a tablet and a laptop are all kinds of ___.","answer":"devices","explanation":"Device.","explanation_ar":"أجهزة.","options":["devices","passwords","updates","messages"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"My cousin lives in Dammam, but we play chess ___ every Friday.","answer":"online","explanation":"Online.","explanation_ar":"عبر الإنترنت.","options":["digital","battery","online","privacy"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Please ___ the phone to the car speaker so that we can all hear the recitation.","answer":"connect","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"وصّل.","options":["download","ignore","connect","update"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Our school library now lends e-books on tablets, so it is a ___ library too.","answer":"digital","explanation":"Digital.","explanation_ar":"رقمية.","options":["private","careful","noisy","digital"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"We lost the internet ___ during the sandstorm.","answer":"connection","explanation":"After *the internet* + noun → noun.","explanation_ar":"الاتصال.","options":["connect","connection","connected","connects"],"dok":2,"skill":"word families","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The robot arm in the car factory is very ___; it paints every car perfectly.","answer":"reliable","explanation":"After *very* → adjective.","explanation_ar":"موثوق.","options":["rely","reliable","reliance","unreliably"],"dok":2,"skill":"word families","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*With a library card, students have **access** to thousands of e-books.* *Access* means ___.","answer":"the chance to use something","explanation":"Context clue.","explanation_ar":"إمكانية الوصول.","options":["a kind of battery","a heavy bag","a broken screen","the chance to use something"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence from a text about school tablets is a FACT?","answer":"A tablet can store hundreds of e-books.","explanation":"Checkable.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","options":["Tablets are the greatest invention ever.","Every student should love tablets.","A tablet can store hundreds of e-books.","Paper books are boring."],"dok":1,"skill":"fact and opinion","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which signal phrase BEST starts the conclusion of a text about robots in schools?","answer":"For these reasons,","explanation":"Signal phrase.","explanation_ar":"For these reasons.","options":["Once upon a time,","For these reasons,","Next,","For example,"],"dok":1,"skill":"signal phrases","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which rule for the school learning app is written correctly?","answer":"Students should log out after every lesson.","explanation":"should + base verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Students should to log out after every lesson.","Students should log out after every lesson.","Students should logs out after every lesson.","Students should logging out after every lesson."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which fact BEST supports the opinion *Children should never share their passwords*?","answer":"Anyone who knows your password can open your account and read your messages.","explanation":"Supporting fact.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة داعمة.","options":["Passwords can have letters and numbers.","Some games have castles.","Many children like gold coins.","Anyone who knows your password can open your account and read your messages."],"dok":2,"skill":"supporting evidence","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Tablets are lighter than paper books, ___ students can carry them more easily.","answer":"so","explanation":"Result.","explanation_ar":"so.","options":["so","but","although","or"],"dok":2,"skill":"linking words","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which is the STRONGEST conclusion for a text saying *Every school should teach online safety*?","answer":"In conclusion, online-safety lessons protect our privacy, our feelings and our friends. Let's ask our school to start this term!","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"أقوى خاتمة.","options":["Also, the internet was invented a long time ago.","That's the end of my text about the internet.","In conclusion, online-safety lessons protect our privacy, our feelings and our friends. Let's ask our school to start this term!","But maybe safety lessons are not needed."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a two-paragraph opinion text (120–150 words) answering the question: *Should students use tablets in class?* Paragraph 1: a hook, your clear opinion and two reasons, each supported by a fact or an example. Paragraph 2: a third reason and a strong conclusion (signal phrase, restated opinion and summary, call to action).","model":"Imagine a school bag that weighs less than one book but holds a whole library! I believe students should use tablets in class, with clear rules. First, tablets make bags lighter, because one tablet can store hundreds of e-books. Second, tablets make lessons more exciting. In science, for example, we can watch a volcano erupt or look inside a plant cell in 3D. (new paragraph) Finally, tablets help us practice at our own speed, since many learning apps give instant feedback. Of course, tablets should be used only for schoolwork during lessons. In conclusion, tablets would make our bags lighter, our lessons richer and our practice smarter. Let's ask our principal to start with one tablet day a week and see the difference!","marks":20}});
